
[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "002.002"]
[White "Preface"]
[Black "Introduction"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The endgame is the best place to sharpen your tactical skills and perfect your piece coordination. With fewer pieces on the board, distractions are minimal, allowing you to focus fully on learning the correct techniques. On top of that, solving endgame exercises is one of the most effective ways to improve your calculation and visualization skills.   About the Series   This course is the second volume in a trilogy designed to teach you the most important tactical tools in the endgame and, more importantly, to ensure you can apply them in your own games. The first volume, 1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Beginners, introduced the fundamental tactical
(mating)
patterns and highlighted the importance of preparatory moves. This second volume builds on that foundation with deeper and more complex combinations, while also introducing endgame-specific techniques such as pawn promotion and stalemate traps. Naturally, there is also a strong emphasis on defence, as every well-rounded player must be able to both attack and defend effectively.   The trilogy ends with 1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Advanced Club Players.  A Gradual Increase in Complexity   The course is divided into four parts. We begin with pure pawn endings in Part I, then progress from single-piece tactics in Part II to two- and three-piece combinations in Parts III and IV. This step-by-step approach enables a gradual increase in complexity, allowing you to build confidence as you progress. This also has the practical advantage that any piece trade will bring us an endgame scenario we have already studied before.   Practical and beautiful   The exercises are drawn from a mix of real games, composed studies, and original positions that I have created for training purposes. I’ve taken care to select not only the most practical and realistic examples, but also the most entertaining ones. Some of my personal favourites are the exercises where you must catch a queen using just two or three pieces. They are not only fun to solve, but also excellent for sharpening your calculation and coordination skills.   Take it slow   My advice is to enjoy the journey and take your time. And always prioritize accuracy over speed. Learning new skills is never easy, and progress takes time. To support your learning, each part includes flash cards to summarize key techniques and tests to check your understanding. These tests combine all the topics you’ve covered so far, helping you practise without knowing in advance what to expect. Just like in your own games.   If you have any feedback or stories you'd like to share, feel free to reach out to me at chesstoolbox@outlook.com.   Thomas Willemze  Haarlem, July 2025.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "002.003"]
[White "Preparatory moves"]
[Black "Introduction"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{Most of the exercises you’re about to tackle in this course are three, four, or even more moves deep. This means that simply recognizing a tactical pattern is rarely enough. You’ll often need to prepare the tactic first, which involves manoeuvring your pawns and pieces onto the right squares to make the tactic work.   Targeting, Chasing, and Luring   The key technical tools for this kind of preparation are targeting, chasing, and luring. While I hope you’re already familiar with these techniques, they’re worth revisiting here, as they appear throughout every chapter of this course. To explain them as clearly as possible, I’ll use three examples from the queen versus rook endgame. This is a setting that is perfect for highlighting these ideas in their purest form.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "002.004"]
[White "Targeting"]
[Black "Introduction"]
[FEN "2K5/8/2k5/1p1ppp2/8/5P1Q/pr6/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{We start with our first preparatory technique: targeting. The queen uses an attack on the rook or the king to reach its optimal square with tempo.   When solving tactical exercises like this one, a good starting point is to identify potential targets. You may have noticed that the black rook is unprotected and the black king is slightly exposed. If we combine these two elements, we can see that c1 emerges as a promising square for the queen. From this square, both the unprotected rook and the king come under threat. And there’s more good news: the queen can reach c1 with tempo!} 1. Qh6+ {[%cal Gh6c6,Gh6c1,Gc1b2,Gc1c5,Rc6c5][%csl Gc1,Rb2,Rc6,Rc5]This move attacks the king and threatens to reach the desired c1-square.} Kc5 {The black king is unable to leave the c-file and avoid our tactic.} 2. Qc1+ {[%cal Gc1b2,Gc1c5][%csl Rc5,Rb2]The double attack decides the game.} Kb4 {} 3. Qxb2+ {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "002.005"]
[White "Chasing"]
[Black "Introduction"]
[FEN "4K3/2p5/3k4/1p1pp1Q1/8/5P2/pr6/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{With chasing, the queen forces an enemy piece
(mostly the king)
to a square where it can be the target of a new combination. This can either be a direct double attack
(the final goal)
, or another check to bring the queen closer to the optimal square.  In the previous example, we used the targeting technique to manoeuvre our queen to c1. This position allows the queen to reach c1 in one move, but there is no black king at the c-file yet and therefore no double attack. The solution is to chase the king to the c-file first and only then move our queen to c1.} 1. Qh6+ {[%cal Gh6d6,Rd6c5,Gh6c1,Gc1b2,Gc1c5][%csl Rd6,Rc5,Rb2,Gc1]} Kc5 {The only legal move.} 2. Qc1+ {[%cal Gc1b2,Gc1c5][%csl Rb2,Rc5]This double attack decides the game.} Kd4 {} 3. Qxb2+ {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "002.006"]
[White "Luring"]
[Black "Introduction"]
[FEN "3K4/8/2pkp3/1p1p4/2P5/5P2/pr6/7Q w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The luring technique forces an enemy piece to a bad square where it can be exploited by a tactic. It is a preparatory move that involves a pawn or piece sacrifice, or exchange.   In this position, the preparatory move is a pawn sacrifice that lures the king to the c-file with tempo. Next, the queen gets to c1 in one move to execute the double attack on the rook and king.} 1. c5+ {[%cal Rd6c5,Gh1c1,Gc1b2,Gc1c5][%csl Rc5,Rb2]} Kxc5 {1...Ke5
would have allowed White to execute a successful skewer with:
2.Qh8+!
.} 2. Qc1+ {[%cal Gc1b2,Gc1c5][%csl Rb2,Rc5]Once again, the double attack decides the game.} Kd4 {} 3. Qxb2+ {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "002.007"]
[White "Conclusion"]
[Black "Introduction"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{Targeting, chasing, and luring are essential preparatory techniques for solving longer and more complex exercises. Targeting is aimed at improving our own piece with tempo, while both chasing and luring are designed to force an enemy piece to a square of our choice.   Make sure you master these techniques really well as they are required for solving most of the exercises in this book. Whenever you are in doubt on any of these techniques, I recommend that you return to this Introduction and review the examples once again.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "003.002"]
[White "Test Prior Knowledge"]
[Black "Test Prior Knowledge"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The 1001 Chess Endgame Exercises curriculum consists of three courses. The first volume, 1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Beginners, teaches you all the tactical
(mating)
patterns that you must know and introduces preparatory moves. This second volume takes the same tactical patterns and preparatory moves to a higher level, pushing your calculation horizon with deeper and more complex exercises.   Is This the Right Level for You?   If you’re unsure whether this book is the right starting point, or whether you should begin with the first volume, I’ve created a short diagnostic test to help assess your current level. The test contains 60 exercises. If you can solve at least 48 of them with relative ease, you’re ready to dive into this volume. In that case, you can treat the test as both a refresher of key tactical tools and an extra training session to reinforce your skills. If, however, you find yourself struggling to reach this 80% mark, I recommend starting with the first volume to ensure you build on a solid foundation.   Good luck!} 1. -- {} 


