[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "006.002"]
[White "Introduction - Pure Pawn Endings - The Breakthrough"]
[Black "1.2 Pure Pawn Endings - The Breakthrough"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The aim of a pawn breakthrough is to create a passed pawn and eventually promote it into a queen. It is a high-impact technique that will improve our results in the endgame. You can imagine how learning to exploit weaknesses in your opponent's pawn structure can also be very rewarding in the opening and middle game stages.   Breakthrough techniques  In this Introduction, we will explore in variations 1-4 how breakthroughs often have to be foreseen in advance, just before liquidating into a pawn ending. I will explain how a successful breakthrough often involves attacking the base of a pawn chain and, in some cases, fixing it first. We will also discuss a more complex example in which the opponent still has a fluid pawn structure.  Neutralize the defending king   We finish this introduction with three examples
(variation 5a-5c)
in which we must neutralize a defending king by blocking it, by using a second passed pawn to our advantage, or by applying the zugzwang technique to decide the game.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "006.003"]
[White "1. Liquidating into a winning pawn ending"]
[Black "1.2 Pure Pawn Endings - The Breakthrough"]
[FEN "1k6/pp3pp1/2r1pb1p/7P/2P2PP1/1PB5/P7/1K1R4 w - - 0 31"]
[Result "*"]

{Pawn breakthroughs can be very straightforward when seen in practice, but we should remember that the complexity is often caused by the fact that it must be foreseen in advance. For instance, when we are about to trade off the remaining pieces, as was the case in this example.  This typical Caro-Kann ending arose in the game between Kardelen Cemhan
(1813)
and Tatia Kokhreidze
(1569)
that was played at the 2010 European Under 14 Girls Championships in Batumi. White, who is enjoying a pleasant space advantage on the kingside, was up to the task and spotted an opportunity to liquidate into a winning pawn ending.} 31. Bxf6 {[%cal Gd1d7,Gd7f7][%csl Gd7,Rf7]} gxf6 {} 32. Rd7 {[%cal Gd7f7][%csl Rf7]Bringing the rook to the seventh rank is usually the aim of controlling an open file. This powerful move attacks the f7-pawn and provokes Black's next move.
32.Rd8+
Kc7
33.Rf8
would also have been strong.} Rc7 {} 33. Rxc7 {[%cal Gg4g5,Gh5h8,Rh6g5][%csl Gh8]It might feel unnatural to give up our strong rook, but we should always focus on the things that remain when considering a piece trade.} Kxc7 {This is the pawn ending that White must have envisioned on move 31. Back then, she must have asked herself the three important questions:   1) Can I create a passed pawn?   2) Can this pawn be stopped by the enemy king?   3) Will my passed pawn promote first?   The last question is important because many breakthroughs also give our opponent a passed pawn, and we should always check if we can stop this pawn with our king or promote first.} 34. g5 {[%cal Gh5h8,Rh6g5][%csl Gh8]This pawn sacrifice forces a breakthrough and is the critical move that White had to find. The key technique that White applies is to identify and attack the base of our opponent's pawn chain in order to destabilize it. You can regard the h6-pawn as a very tiny pawn chain consisting of only one pawn. The fact that it cannot be protected by any other pawn makes it very vulnerable to a breakthrough.} fxg5 {} 35. fxg5 {This is the position that White was aiming at. It has become clear that Black cannot prevent the breakthrough as either the white g- or h-pawn is about to reach the desired h8-square. Note that if Black were allowed to move the f7-pawn to g7, this would change everything, as this g7-pawn would be the new base of the pawn chain, and we wouldn't be able to attack it.} hxg5 {35...Kd7
was the second move that needed to be checked. Is the enemy king close enough to stop our pawn? The answer was no because the king will be outside the pawn's square, which means that the pawn queens after
36.gxh6
Ke7
37.h7
Kf8
38.h8=Q+
.} 36. h6 {[%cal Gh6h8][%csl Gh8]The white h-pawn is much quicker than the opponent's passed pawn, which is why Black resigned in the game.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "006.004"]
[White "2. Attacking the pawn base"]
[Black "1.2 Pure Pawn Endings - The Breakthrough"]
[FEN "7k/4p3/3pPp2/2pP4/1pP5/1P6/P7/K7 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{This cute study, composed by Ban in 1954, is a more elaborate example of attacking the base. The pawn chain is now more visible, running from e7 all the way up to b4. The e7-pawn is our target and we must be ready to sacrifice several pawns to attack it.} 1. a4 {[%cal Rb4a3,Gb3b4,Gb4c5][%csl Rc5]} bxa3 {1...Kg8
was the alternative that we had to check. Luckily, the king is still outside the pawn's square and we win easily after
2.a5
Kf8
3.a6
Ke8
4.a7
.} 2. b4 {[%cal Rc5b4,Gc4c5,Gc5d6][%csl Rd6]} cxb4 {2...Kg8
3.b5
is, again, hopeless for Black.} 3. c5 {[%cal Rd6c5,Gd5d6,Gd6e7][%csl Re7]} dxc5 {} 4. d6 {[%cal Re7d6,Ge6e8][%csl Ge8]} exd6 {4...Kg7
5.dxe7
wins as well.} 5. e7 {[%cal Ge7e8][%csl Ge8]It is game over. The black pawns are no match for our future queen.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "006.005"]
[White "3. Fixing the pawn base"]
[Black "1.2 Pure Pawn Endings - The Breakthrough"]
[FEN "8/1kp2p2/1p2p2p/4P3/6PP/8/2K5/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{Attacking the base of the pawn chain that is holding back our potential passed pawn is a major step in creating a breakthrough, but we should understand that this technique usually only works when we fix the base first. With fixing, I mean that we should block the pawn to make sure it can no longer advance.  I will illustrate this with a simple example.   This position is very similar to the Caro-Kann ending we discussed before, but the fact that our h-pawn is less advanced and currently not blocking the black h-pawn requires us to insert an extra step in the process.} 1. h5 {[%cal Gg4g5,Rh6g5,Gh5h8][%csl Rh6,Gh8]Fixing the base is the techique that we need to use.
Attacking the base instead would be the wrong tool in this position as
1.g5
enables Black to turn the tables with
1...h5!
. The base has shifted and we are no longer able to attack it. Black is suddenly winning due to the two connected passed queenside pawns.} Kc6 {Bringing over the king is Black's only chance, but it does not help because it will be outside the pawn's square.} 2. g5 {[%cal Rh6g5,Gh5h8][%csl Gh8]This time, we are ready to attack the base.} hxg5 {2...Kd7
3.gxh6
would also place the black king outside the square.} 3. h6 {[%cal Gh6h8][%csl Gh8]} g4 {Always remember to involve your opponent's passed pawn into your calculations. There is no need to worry in this position because our pawn queens much faster and we also have a king within the pawn's square that is able to stop it if required.} 4. h7 {4.Kd2
g3
5.Ke2
g2
6.Kf2
would be the alternative way to stop the pawn.} g3 {} 5. h8=Q {} g2 {} 6. Qh2 {} 


