Championships in Bratislava.  Creating a pawn breakthrough is not so simple, as it is yet unclear which white pawn must be fixed and subsequently attacked. In this case, it really helps when you have studied this example before, and it is impressive to see how Black already knew his classics at such a young age!} 39. b3 {[%cal Rc2b3,Rb2a3,Ga4a3,Gc4c1]It turns out we need to fix the b2-pawn to reach our goal. The fact that this pawn can be attacked in two different ways is a key ingredient for our success.
39...a3
40.bxa3
bxa3
41.g5
and
39...c3
40.bxc3
bxc3
41.g5
wins for White.} 40. cxb3 {This is an important moment. The b2-pawn is now fixed (by a white pawn), which means that we can attack it.
40.axb3
c3!
is similar to the main line.} a3 {[%cal Rb2a3,Gc4c1][%csl Rb2,Gc1]White resigned in view of} 41. bxa3 {} c3 {[%cal Gc3c1][%csl Gc1]Black wins.} 


