{So far, the enemy king did not play a role and we could focus entirely on breaking through with our pawns. In case the monarch stands within the pawn's square and is close enough to stop it, there are three things we can do:   - Obstruct the king;   - Use a second pawn;   - Create a zugzwang situation.   We start with creating an obstruction. This position is the final phase of a 2014 study composed by Sayman and Oktem. A closer look reveals that we already have a passed pawn in place, but that the enemy king is within the pawn's square. This means that we cannot just push our pawn, but have to block the enemy king along the way.} 6. h4 {[%cal Gh4h8][%csl Gh8]It is important to start with this move to lure the king to d5 first.
6.e6
fxe6
7.f6
exf6
creates a blockade, but enables the black king to get around it with
8.h4
Kd6
9.h5
Ke7
10.h6
Kf7
and stop our pawn after all.   Always remember that finding the right timing is a crucial skill in chess!} Kd5 {[%cal Gd5e5,Ge5h8,Rh4h8][%csl Gh8]This is an important moment in the study as it teaches us something very important about blockades.   The most effective blockades are the ones constructed from our opponent's own pawns and pieces!   In this case, we must use the black pawns for our purpose.} 7. e6 {[%cal Rf7e6,Gf5f6,Re7f6,Gh4h8][%csl Re6,Rf6]7.h5
Kxe5
8.h6
Kf6
9.Kxa2
e6
10.fxe6
fxe6
is only a draw.
7.f6
is an instructive mistake.
7...e6!
Our own pawns cannot stop the enemy king! They will simply be eaten after
8.h5
Kxe5
9.h6
Kxf6
10.h7
Kg7
and Black wins.} fxe6 {} 8. f6 {[%cal Re7f6,Gh4h8][%csl Rf6,Gh8]This is the key move. We are about to create a blockade with black pawns that prevent the enemy king from catching up with our pawn.
8.h5
would spoil everything after
8...Ke5
9.h6
Kf6
.} exf6 {} 9. h5 {[%cal Gh5h8,Rd5e5,Re5h8][%csl Gh8,Rf6]} Ke5 {} 10. h6 {[%cal Gh6h8,Re5h8][%csl Gh8,Rf6]Mission accomplished! The black king does not have the f6-square available which means that our pawn promotes.} 


