{This is the third and final example in which we prevent the enemy king from stopping our passed pawn. This time, we will use zugzwang to reach our goal.   The black king is as remote as it can get from the queenside and also occupied with blocking the white h-pawn. It will, however, play an important role in promoting one of our own pawns into a queen!} 1. a4 {[%cal Gb5b4,Ra3b4,Ga4a1][%csl Ga1]We already know the first step we need to take: fix the base!} 2. Kd5 {} b4 {[%cal Ra3b4,Ga4a1][%csl Ga1]Next, we attack the base!} 3. cxb4 {} cxb4 {[%cal Ra3b4,Ga4a1][%csl Ga1]} 4. Kc4 {[%cal Gb4a3,Gc4c3,Gc3a1][%csl Ga1]Bringing in the king as fast as possible is White's most logical attempt.
4.axb4
a3
puts the white king outside the pawn's square and is an easy win for Black after, for instance:
5.b5
a2
6.b6
a1=Q
7.Kc6
Qc3+
8.Kd7
Qxc2
.} bxa3 {} 5. Kc3 {[%cal Ga3a1]This configuration with two passed a-pawns and a defending king on c3 occurs quite frequently and is therefore important to remember. Although the pawns cannot go any further, the defending king cannot come any closer either! This is where our king can decide the game, as it helps us to pass the move to the opponent without making a concession.} Kg8 {[%csl Rc3]It is zugzwang!
Note that without a king move, we would be in zugzwang ourselves as
5...a2
would cost us both our a-pawns after
6.Kb2
. A situation in which both players cannot make a move without worsening their position is called a reciprocal zugzwang.} 6. h7+ {} Kxh7 {[%csl Rc3]And another zugzwang. White is now forced to move the king, after which the a-pawn decides the game.} 7. Kd3 {} a2 {[%cal Ga2a1][%csl Ga1]} 


