{The previous section taught us many techniques that we could use to force a successful breakthrough. It is now time to take another point of view and learn how we can defend ourselves against these hostilities.   Switching perspectives is one of my favourite training methods as it helps us to get a clearer and more objective view on a certain topic. Most chess books focus on the attack, but I believe that the defence is equally important and also requires skills that need to be trained. This section teaches us three important lessons that we can use to arm ourselves against a powerful breakthrough in the pawn ending.   The first one is a warning against moving the base of your pawn chain. Variation 1 features one of many practical examples in which a player pushed this pawn too ambitiously and got punished badly.   Next, I will show you two different pawn configurations that you can create to bolster your pawn chain and prevent our opponent from breaking through: the triangle and the bowl. They will be the subject of the variations 2a and 2b.   Last but not least, we will discover in variation 3 how moving the king into the pawn's square can be a very effective method to keep the game balanced.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "007.003"]
[White "1. Don't move your pawn base"]
[Black "1.3 Pure Pawn Endings - Defending against Breakthrough"]
[FEN "8/p7/1pp5/4k1pp/PPP1P1p1/4K1P1/7P/8 b - - 0 35"]
[Result "*"]

{This example is a warning against unnecessarily weakening your pawn structure in pawn endings. In particular, be very careful not to move the base of the chain without triple-checking the breakthrough opportunities for your opponent. The following anonymized game is just one of dozens of examples that I found in the Megadatabase where a player got punished for moving the base of his pawn chain.
Not much is going on here, and a draw would be the most likely result after a move like
35...c5
, or
35...Kd6
as neither side can make any progress. However, this evaluation changes rapidly after the next move.} 35. a5 {This might look like a logical move at first sight, with the harmless
36.bxa5
bxa5
as a logical follow-up. However, we already know that this pawn was the base of the pawn chain. Was, because this role has now been taken over by the b-pawn! And this pawn is more advanced and therefore much more susceptible to a breakthrough as the game continuation shows.} 36. c5 {[%cal Rb6c5,Gb4a5,Ga5a8][%csl Ga8]Well played! White immediately attacks the base, forcing a deadly breakthrough.} b5 {Black creates a passed pawn of his own, but it will be too slow and also too close to the white king.
36...bxc5
would not have helped either, because the second passed pawn decides the game after
37.bxa5
Kd6
38.a6
Kc7
39.a7
Kb7
40.e5
Kxa7
41.e6
.} 37. bxa5 {[%cal Ga5a8][%csl Ga8]} b4 {} 38. a6 {[%cal Ga6a8][%csl Ga8]} b3 {} 39. Kd3 {[%cal Gd3b1,Rb3b1,Ga6a8][%csl Gb1,Ga8]The king steps within the pawn's square and the a-pawn promoted a few moves later after which Black resigned.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "007.004"]
[White "2a. The Triangle"]
[Black "1.3 Pure Pawn Endings - Defending against Breakthrough"]
[FEN "8/5ppp/8/5PPP/8/8/K1k5/8 b - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{Instead of weakening our own pawn structure by moving the base of the pawn chain, we should try to bolster our structure by choosing the most solid configurations possible. I will show you two highly secure set-ups.   The first set-up is designed to arm us against a very famous breakthrough. One that should look familiar by now. White threatens a deadly breakthrough, and the only way to stop this play is by creating a triangle with} 1. g6 {[%cal Gf7g6,Gh7g6][%csl Gg6]Remember this pawn structure well, as it is the most solid pawn structure we can get against breakthrough. Our structure has the support of two different pawn bases that cannot be easily attacked.
Instead,
1...Kc3
2.g6
fxg6
3.h6
gxh6
4.f6
was the line that we had to avoid!
1...h6
2.f6
is also an easy win for White.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "007.005"]
[White "2b. The Bowl"]
[Black "1.3 Pure Pawn Endings - Defending against Breakthrough"]
[FEN "8/k7/5pp1/7p/5PPP/8/8/3K4 b - - 0 38"]
[Result "*"]

{This is the same pawn configuration as in the first example when Black deliberately weakened the structure with 1...a5. Luckily for us, we are to move and can still repair the pawn structure.   Creating a triangle is not possible with the pawn already on h5. In that case, we can try to create a pawn chain that resembles a bowl.} 38. f5 {[%cal Rf4f5,Rh4h5][%csl Gg6,Gh5,Gf5]We only have one pawn base, but the sides of the bowl guarantee that our base cannot be attacked and is therefore able to keep the chain solid.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "007.006"]
[White "3. Moving the king inside the pawn's square"]
