{It is now time to increase the level of complexity by giving our opponent an extra minor piece to stop our pawn from promoting.   The fight between pawns and a minor piece is fascinating and forces both players to be creative and calculate well. It is, therefore, an excellent learning environment for our endgame tactics training. This chapter features the fight between pawns against a bishop
(and pawns)
and pawns against a knight. We will again switch perspectives along the way to learn the most important techniques for both sides.   This leads to the following table of contents:   2.1 Pawns vs bishop  2.2 Defend with your bishop  2.3 Pawns vs knight  2.4 Defend with your knight  Each section ends with a set of exercises that you can use to internalize the different techniques.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.002"]
[White "Introduction - Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[Black "2.1 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{We continue our journey with positions where our opponent can use an extra bishop to stop our pawns. The bishop is a long-range piece and, therefore, capable of ruining our plans from a large distance. The most important technique that we can use to queen our pawn is therefore to block one or more diagonals to neutralize this powerful piece.   We already know that the most effective blockades are the ones that are built from our opponent's own pawns or pieces. This section features two examples in which we will  use these types of blockade.  In the first one, we lure the enemy king to a clumsy square, whereas in the second one, we ensure our opponent's own pawn will be the one to block the bishop's view.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.003"]
[White "1. Using the enemy king for a blockade"]
[Black "2.1 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[FEN "8/8/P7/5k1b/8/3K2P1/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{This is the starting position of a study that was composed by Mason in 1894. Our a-pawn is very close to promoting, but we cannot simply push it because the bishop is a long-range piece and could easily stop it by stepping on the a8-h1 diagonal. What we need to do is to create a road block that would hamper the bishop in its mobility and prevent it from reaching the desired diagonal.} 1. g4+ {[%cal Rh5g4,Ga6a8,Gd3e3,Ge3f3,Gg4f3,Gg4c8][%csl Ga8,Rf3,Rf5,Gc8]This pawn sacrifice lures either the king or the bishop to g4 and disturbs the coordination between the two pieces.
It is instructive to see how both
1.a7
Bf3
and
1.Ke3
Be8
2.a7
Bc6
would enable Black to defend without much effort as the bishop reaches the desired diagonal without any trouble. This changes radically after our clever pawn sacrifice.} Bxg4 {You can already see how the king is standing in the bishop's way.
However, capturing the pawn with the king would have led to an even quicker defeat as the pawn is already unstoppable after
1...Kxg4
2.a7
. The bishop cannot enter the f3-square, meaning it is game over.} 2. Ke3 {[%cal Ge3f3,Gg4c8,Ga6a8][%csl Rf3,Rf5,Ga8]This was the point. The enemy king is blocking the bishop's view and preventing it from reaching the long diagonal via the back rank. The bishop has only one option left, which means we can use our king to decide the game.} Bh3 {} 3. Kf2 {[%cal Gf2g2,Gh3g2,Ga6a8,Gh3c8][%csl Rg2,Ra8,Rf5]The king controls both g2 and f3, making it impossible for the bishop to control the a8-square in time. Our pawn will promote within two moves and decide the game.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.004"]
[White "2. Using your opponent's pawn for a blockade"]
[Black "2.1 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[FEN "k7/8/7P/4p3/8/8/3PK3/b7 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The previous example taught us how we can lure the enemy king to an uncomfortable square where it blocked its own bishop. It is now time to use one of our opponent's pawns to create an effective blockade.   As you can see, we again possess a passed pawn that is very close to the other side of the board. Our job is to prevent our opponent from opening up the long diagonal with ...d5-d4 as this would enable the bishop to stop our pawn from promoting.} 1. d4 {[%cal Ge5d4,Ge2d3,Gh6h8,Ga1h8][%csl Rd3,Gh8,Rd4]We again make use of the luring technique. Black has the ugly choice between bringing the bishop or the pawn closer to our king.} Bxd4 {1...exd4
2.Kd3!
would be an even quicker win for White. The pawn is blocked, the bishop obstructed, meaning our pawn will become a queen soon.} 2. Kd3 {[%cal Gd3d4,Re5e4,Gh6h8][%csl Rh8]This is an important technique to remember. By attacking the bishop with our king, we paralyse the black pawn for the moment and force our opponent to spend a move on bringing the bishop into safety.} Bb2 {The bishop is safe now and Black does again threaten to open up the diagonal with ... e5-e4. It is, however, our turn first!} 3. Ke4 {[%cal Re5e4,Gb2h8,Gh6h8][%csl Re4,Re5,Gh8]The road block is in place. The bishop is seriously hampered by its own colleague and cannot prevent our pawn from deciding the game.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.005"]
[White "Conclusion - Pawns against a piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[Black "2.1 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{Blocking the bishop's diagonals is a very important technique to master if you want to prevent the enemy bishop from stopping your passed pawn. The most effective blockades are those in which we use our opponent's king or pawns to shut the bishop out.   Make sure you have this technique ready when solving the exercises and be ready to use the tools from the previous chapter as well!   Good luck!} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.006"]
[White "Exercise 103 - Pawns vs Bishop"]
[Black "2.1 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[FEN "8/3K2p1/1k3p2/5PP1/7P/8/7b/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

1. h5 {A classic breakthrough. The pawn is on its way to attack the base.
1.Ke6
fxg5
2.hxg5
Bf4
3.f6
gxf6
4.gxf6
Bh6
is an easy draw for Black.
1.gxf6
gxf6
doesn't work either as the black king and bishop are in time to stop the pawns. For instance after
2.h5
Bf4
3.Ke6
Bg5
.} fxg5 {1...Be5
2.h6!
and not:
2.g6
Bf4!
fxg5
3.h7!
g6
4.Ke6
Bh8
5.f6!
...f6-f7 is coming and White wins.} 2. f6 {} gxf6 {} 3. h6 {} Be5 {The best attempt. Now we can decide the game by attacking the bishop and blocking the f-pawn.
3...f5
4.Ke6
is an easier win for White.} 4. Ke6 {} Ba1 {} 5. Kf5 {The pawn promotes and White wins. Leenhouts (1922).} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.007"]
[White "Exercise 104 - Pawns vs Bishop"]
[Black "2.1 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[FEN "b7/k2p4/2p5/1P6/2P4P/8/1K6/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

1. b6+ {Our aim is to lure the king to b6 to be able to block the c6-pawn with tempo.
1.h5
cxb5
2.h6
Be4
enables the bishop to stop our pawn and win the game.} Kxb6 {1...Kb8
2.c5
Bb7
3.h5
Ba6
4.Kc3
also wins.} 2. c5+ {} Kxc5 {} 3. h5 {The pawn is unstoppable and White wins. Dawson (1927).} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.008"]
[White "Exercise 105 - Pawns vs Bishop"]
[Black "2.1 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[FEN "8/8/3pp1P1/3b4/5k2/3P2p1/4P3/4K3 w - - 0 3"]
[Result "*"]

3. e4 {Our aim is to block the e6-pawn to keep the a2-g8 diagonal closed.} Ba2 {} 4. e5 {} dxe5 {} 5. g7 {Kopac (1974).} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.009"]
[White "Exercise 106 - Pawns vs Bishop"]
[Black "2.1 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[FEN "8/8/7P/8/1p6/kP6/1bP5/1K6 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

1. c4 {This move gives Black the unpleasant choice between coping with two passed pawns and blocking their own bishop.
1.h7
Bc3!
wins for Black!} bxc3 {1...Kxb3
2.c5
would also be hopeless as the bishop cannot stop both pawns.} 2. Kc2 {The bishop has been neutralized and the h-pawn will queen. Efron (1957).} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.010"]
[White "Exercise 107 - Pawns vs Bishop"]
[Black "2.1 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[FEN "8/P1p1kp2/8/3p4/8/2K5/4P1b1/8 w - - 0 3"]
[Result "*"]

3. e4 {This move is the only way to victory.
3.a8=Q
runs into a nasty discovered attack after
3...d4+
4.Kxd4
Bxa8
, whereas
3.Kd4
c5+
4.Kxc5
d4
5.Kxd4
Bb7
is only a draw.} Bxe4 {} 4. Kd4 {} c5+ {} 5. Ke5 {White continuously has to come up with precise moves. We are almost there.
5.Ke3
Bh1
6.a8=Q
d4+
would run into the same trick that I showed before.} f6+ {} 6. Kf4 {Black has run out of checks which means that the bishop is unable to stop the pawn. White wins. Bondarenko & Kakovin (1955).} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.011"]
[White "Exercise 108 - Pawns vs Bishop"]
[Black "2.1 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[FEN "8/p7/7P/bP1K4/8/7k/8/8 w - - 0 2"]
[Result "*"]

2. b6 {White lures the black pawn to b6 to create a blockade on the a5-d8 diagonal.} axb6 {2...Bxb6
3.h7
would be game over as the bishop is unable to stop our pawn.} 3. Kd4 {The king controls the important c3-square which prevents the bishop from entering the long diagonal.
We must avoid
3.Kc4
on account of
3...b5+!
4.Kxb5
4.Kd3
Bd8
5.h7
Bf6
Bc3
, with a draw.} Bd2 {} 4. h7 {} Bc1 {} 5. h8=Q+ {The queen promotes with check and White wins.
Note that
5.Kc3!
would be the most thematic move and wins as well. Leenhouts (1919).} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.012"]
[White "Exercise 109 - Pawns vs Bishop"]
[Black "2.1 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[FEN "8/8/1b1p3P/8/k1P1p1p1/8/2P1P3/5K2 w - - 0 3"]
[Result "*"]

3. c5 {First we lure either the bishop or the d-pawn to c5 to make sure the bishop can no longer reach the long diagonal in two different ways.
Both
3.h7
Bd4
and
3.c3
Bd8
4.h7
Bf6
make life too easy for Black.} Bxc5 {The only try as the bishop is too late after
3...dxc5
4.h7
and unable to stop two pawns after
3...Bd8
4.h7
Bf6
5.c6
.} 4. c3 {The pawn controls d4 and White wins.} Be3 {} 5. h7 {Zepler (1916).} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.013"]
[White "Exercise 110 - Pawns vs Bishop"]
[Black "2.1 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[FEN "8/pp6/3p4/PPPp1k2/1b3P2/5K2/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

1. a6 {We first want the b-pawn out of the way before we advance our b-pawn.
Both
1.c6
Bxa5!
2.cxb7
Bc7
and
1.b6
Bxc5
2.a6
Bxb6
3.axb7
Bc7
win for Black.} bxa6 {} 2. b6 {} axb6 {2...Ba5
3.bxa7!
2...Bxc5
3.b7
.} 3. c6 {After this clever move, we have two different road blocks in place that shut out the bishop. Black's last chance is to bring in the king.} Ke6 {} 4. f5+ {But the second passed pawn decides the game!} Ke7 {} 5. f6+ {} Kxf6 {} 6. c7 {Prokes (1950).} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.014"]
[White "Exercise 111 - Pawns vs Bishop"]
