3. d5 {Our aim is to lure the bishop to d5 to be able to advance our c-pawn with tempo.
3.c4
is too slow and loses after
3...Kb3!
4.a7
4.Kd2
Kxc4
Kc3
5.a8=Q
d2+
6.Kd1
Bf3
.} Bxd5 {This is the move that we were waiting for, but the alternatives were also very instructive.
The white pawn promotes after both
3...cxd5
4.cxd3
Bf3
5.d4
and
3...d2+
4.Kxd2
cxd5
5.Ke3!
Bh1
6.Kd4
.} 4. c4 {The pawn moves with tempo and...} Bf3 {} 5. c5 {blocks the c6-pawn! With the long diagonal closed and the king outside the pawn's square, Black can only sit and wait for our a-pawn to decide the game. Troitzky (1914).} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.017"]
[White "Exercise 114 - Pawns vs Bishop"]
[Black "2.1 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[FEN "8/6b1/5k2/8/P3K1P1/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

1. a5 {Step 1 is to push the pawn to force the bishop to the a3-f8 diagonal.
1.g5+
looks tempting, but enables Black to stop the pawn:
1...Ke6
2.a5
Bf8
3.Kd4
Bg7+
4.Kc4
Kd7
The black king steps within the pawn's square and equals the game.} Bf8 {Step 2 is to prevent the bishop from entering the long diagonal, and forcing it back to another route.} 2. Kd5 {} Bh6 {And this is where we should recognize a pattern from our Introduction.} 3. g5+ {This move lures either the king or the bishop to a very unpleasant square, after which the bishop is blocked and our pawn will promote.} Bxg5 {3...Kxg5
4.a6
wins even faster.} 4. Ke4 {} Bh4 {} 5. Kf3 {It is game over as the pawn promotes before the bishop reaches the long diagonal. Otten (1891).} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.018"]
[White "Exercise 115 - Pawns vs Bishop"]
[Black "2.1 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[FEN "8/8/6P1/3pP1Pk/8/8/8/1b4K1 w - - 0 2"]
[Result "*"]

2. g7 {The three white passed pawns are about to march in harmony towards the other side of the board.} Bh7 {} 3. e6 {} Kg6 {} 4. e7 {} Kf7 {It looks as if the black king and bishop have everything under control, but here comes pawn number three.} 5. g6+ {White wins because either the black king or bishop is currently overloaded.} Bxg6 {} 6. e8=Q+ {} Kxe8 {} 7. g8=Q+ {Salai (1978).} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.019"]
[White "Exercise 116 - Pawns vs Bishop"]
