[FEN "5Kbk/8/3pp1P1/7P/4P3/8/8/8 w - - 0 4"]
[Result "*"]

4. e5 {This clever move blocks the e6-pawn and prevents the bishop from defending from a safe distance.
4.h6
e5
5.h7
Bb3
is only a draw as
6.g7+
would now even lose after
6...Kxh7!
.} dxe5 {With the bishop locked in, we can now execute our plan to mate the king.} 5. h6 {} e4 {5...Bh7
6.g7!} 6. h7 {} Bxh7 {} 7. g7 {Motor (1988).} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.021"]
[White "Exercise 118 - Pawns vs Bishop"]
[Black "2.1 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[FEN "8/p7/5p2/1PP2pk1/8/8/5P2/3K2b1 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

1. b6 {1.f4+
does not work on account of
1...Kxf4
2.b6
Bxc5!
3.b7
Bd6
and Black wins.} axb6 {} 2. f4+ {This is the right move order. The b8-h2 diagonal will soon be blocked, after which our pawn decides the game.} Kxf4 {} 3. c6 {Bernhardt (1962).} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.022"]
[White "Exercise 119 - Pawns vs Bishop"]
[Black "2.1 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[FEN "5b2/8/2p5/4P3/2PP4/8/8/K1k5 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

1. c5 {Our pawns are already quite advanced and the bishop can only stop them if it can raise a blockade along one single diagonal. We can prevent this by combining a passed c- and e-pawn.
1.Ka2
looks logical, but enables Black to defend with the precise
1...c5!
. After
2.d5
Kc2!
we will discover that the bishop can either raise a blockade along the f8-a3  or the d8-h4 diagonal.
3.d6
(the most challenging attempt, but not good enough to win the game)
3.e6
Kc3
3...Kc3
4.d7
Be7
. The pawns are blocked and Black wins.} Bg7 {1...Kc2
2.e6
Kc3
3.d5
Bxc5
4.dxc6
is very similar to the main line.} 2. Ka2 {It was very important to slow down for a moment and improve our king before continuing our plan.
2.e6
Bxd4+!
ruins everything for us because Black captures the pawn with check!} Kc2 {} 3. e6 {} Bf8 {3...Bf6
4.d5
cxd5
5.c6
wins for White.} 4. d5 {We are almost there. Soon we will have passed pawns that are advanced enough to force the bishop to stop them along two different diagonals.} cxd5 {} 5. c6 {} Bd6 {The bishop controls both c7 and e7, but the fact that it is overloaded makes life very easy for us.} 6. c7 {or
6.e7
Bxe7
7.c7
.} Bxc7 {} 7. e7 {The pawn promotes and White wins. Prokes (1946).} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "009.023"]
[White "Exercise 120 - Pawns vs Bishop"]
[Black "2.1 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Bishop"]
[FEN "3b4/3P4/3p4/1P1k1P2/8/3P4/8/3K4 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

1. d4 {The symmetry in this position is true art in my opinion. Black has two pieces that are currently holding back White's three passed pawns, but cannot make any progress, while White threatens to advance the king and decide the game.} Kxd4 {This move loses, but
1...Bf6
2.Ke2
Bd8
3.Kf3
does not solve anything for Black either. The white king will simply march to g6 and prepare f5-f6-f7.} 2. f6 {This was the point. Without the help of the black king, the bishop is no match for our three passed pawns.
2.b6!
, with the same idea, would be equally strong.} Bxf6 {} 3. b6 {} Bd8 {} 4. b7 {} Bc7 {The bishop controls both promotion squares, but is overloaded and therefore unable to save the game.} 5. b8=Q {or
5.d8=Q!
Bxd8
6.b8=Q
leads to the same result.} Bxb8 {} 6. d8=Q {Brodd (1915).} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "010.002"]
[White "Introduction - Pawns against a Piece - The Bishop Defends"]
[Black "2.2 Pawns against a Piece - The Bishop Defends "]
[FEN "8/8/8/p2k4/2pP4/2K4p/8/1B6 w - - 0 9"]
[Result "*"]

{It is now time to place ourselves on the other side of the board and see how we can use a bishop to combat a passed pawn. I have created a mix of exercises where we can sometimes achieve success by placing our bishop on the right diagonal or by preventing our opponent from raising a blockade. In other cases, we must accept that we are too late to stop the pawn and should instead look for defensive resources, such as stalemate and perpetual check.   I will show you one example to illustrate what such a drawing combination could look like.  This position arises in the final phase of a study that was composed by Aliev and Almammadov in 2013. The first thing we need to do is to find a way to stop the passed pawn from promoting. Once we are really, really certain that this is impossible, we switch to Plan B and start looking for an inventive way to secure a draw.} 9. Bf5 {[%cal Gf5h3][%csl Rh3]This is the right starting move of a combination that should result in a stalemate. The absence of any pawns that can move and the fact that our king's manoeuvring space is also quite limited should point us in that direction. The next step is to envision a black queen on h1 and pick a square where we want it to be. A clever bishop manoeuvre should do the rest.} h2 {} 10. Bh3 {[%cal Gh2h1,Gh3g2,Gg2d5,Gg2h1][%csl Rh1,Rd5]Our king can only move to the second rank, so that's where we want the enemy queen as well.
Note that stopping the pawn was indeed impossible as
10.Bc8
Kc6
11.d5+
is an easy win for Black after
11...Kb6!
.} h1=Q {This is the position that we had to foresee when starting this exercise. We want the black queen to be on the second rank and we need to get rid of our bishop. Both wishes can be fulfilled in one single move.
Note that 10…
h1=B
prevents the stalemate but is also a draw after
11.Bf1
.} 11. Bg2+ {[%cal Gg2d5,Gg2h1][%csl Rd5,Rh1]This is a double attack, which means that Black is forced to capture our bishop.} Qxg2 {[%cal Gg2a2][%csl Rc3]And it is stalemate!   I hope this example gave you enough inspiration to solve the exercises with confidence and that your own creativity will do the rest. Remember that in some cases, we can simply stop the pawn and win the game. Use the drawing combinations only when you really have to!   Good luck!} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "010.003"]
[White "Exercise 121 - The Bishop Defends"]
[Black "2.2 Pawns against a Piece - The Bishop Defends "]
[FEN "8/2p5/P7/2ppp3/8/K7/2PPP3/k4b2 b - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

1. d4 {Black should waste no time and open up the long diagonal as quickly as possible!
1...Bg2
2.d4!
is what White was hoping for.} 2. a7 {} Bg2 {The pawn cannot advance and Black wins.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
