{We will now replace the bishop with a knight and see how we can still keep queening our pawns with this unpredictable piece around. I will show you two examples that demonstrate the two most important techniques we can use.   In the first example, we learn that a knight can be very clumsy when it nears the side of the board. I will show you how to lure the knight to such a square and how we can exploit its limited mobility.   The second example requires a technique that we already used when combating a bishop: creating a blockade. Only this time, we won't block diagonally, but make sure a certain square becomes unavailable for a knight that is on its way to stop our pawn.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "011.003"]
[White "1. The clumsy knight"]
[Black "2.3 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Knight"]
[FEN "8/4kp2/1p2n1P1/p1p4P/P7/8/1P6/7K w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{Knights can be very effective defenders, but it is important to know that their effectiveness decreases considerably when they near the edges of the board. This example shows that they can even become a serious liability when placed on the wrong square.  It seems like Black has everything under control with a pawn, king, and knight ready to stop our pawns from promoting. However, this changes after our next move.} 1. g7 {[%cal Gg7g8][%csl Gg8]First we sacrifice a pawn to lure the knight to a highly uncomfortable square.} Nxg7 {} 2. h6 {[%cal Gh6g7,Gh6h8][%csl Rg7,Rh8]This is the dream position in any pawns vs knight ending. The knight is in its poorest shape as it is hampered in its movement by the edge of the board, making it impossible to stop the pawn from promoting. And it gets worse. We will soon discover that the knight is also preventing its own king from participating in the defence.} Kf8 {} 3. h7 {[%cal Gh7g8,Rf8g8,Rf8g7,Gh7h8][%csl Rg8,Rg7,Gh8]A sad picture for Black, who can safely resign here. Note that if it weren't for the knight, Black would be winning!} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "011.004"]
[White "2. Blocking a square"]
[Black "2.3 Pawns against a Piece - Pawns against a Knight"]
[FEN "k7/7p/8/1p3PP1/pP2p3/4P3/6n1/3K4 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{White is very close to promoting the f-pawn, but the simplistic 1.f6 would enable the black knight to catch up and block the pawn. What we need to do is to explore the knight's route and find a spot where we can prepare a road block.} 1. g6 {[%cal Rh7g6,Gf5f8,Gg2h4,Gh4g6,Gg6f8][%csl Rg6,Rf8]The following line shows how Black is right in time when we simply start pushing our f-pawn:
1.f6
Nh4
2.f7
Ng6} hxg6 {} 2. f6 {[%cal Gg2h4,Gh4g6,Gg6f8,Gf6f8][%csl Rg6,Gf8]Here we are. While the knight is desperately checking its navigation device for an alternative route, we can simply step aside with our king and march on with our f-pawn to win the game.
2.fxg6
Nxe3+
3.Ke1
Nf5
wins for Black.} Nxe3+ {} 3. Kc1 {[%cal Gf6f8][%csl Gf8]} Ng4 {} 4. f7 {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
