{When defending with our knight against one or more hostile pawns, we can follow a similar approach as we did with the bishop. First, we look for a way to stop the pawn from promoting. If we fail, we try again. Only when we are really certain that our opponent is about to get an extra queen do we switch to Plan B and start looking for resources to force a draw or trick our opponent in some other way.   Earlier, I showed you an example in which we can create a stalemate with our bishop. I will now show you how a knight can be a very suitable piece for levelling the game with a perpetual check.  This is the final phase of a beautiful study that was composed by Sedlak in 1974. Black has already promoted a pawn into a queen, which means that we must act fast.} 3. c8=Q+ {[%cal Gc8a8,Ge8c7][%csl Ra8,Gc7]It looks as if this move just blunders a powerful pawn, but in reality we are clearing the c7-square for our knight.} Qxc8 {} 4. Nc7+ {[%cal Gc7a8][%csl Ra8]This is the start of a pretty combination that combines a perpetual check with a stalemate trap.} Kb8 {The pawn ending that arises after 4…
Qxc7
5.Kxc7
is also equal.} 5. Na6+ {[%cal Ga6b8][%csl Rb8]This is the point of the combination. Black cannot capture our knight, which means we can move it back and forth to continuously attack the enemy king.} Ka8 {Black does not have anything better, since
5...bxa6
leads to a stalemate! It is beautiful to see how Sedlak was able to combine two different drawing mechanisms in one single study.} 6. Nc7+ {[%cal Gc7a8][%csl Ra8]The rest is easy.} Kb8 {} 7. Na6+ {[%cal Ga6b8][%csl Rb8]} bxa6 {[%cal Gc8c1][%csl Rb6]This is the same stalemate as we discussed on move five. It could be an interesting retrograde problem. This means that you can show this diagram to your friends and see if they can determine which moves were played leading up to this position.   Exercises   I have prepared a set of exercises where you can train your perpetual check skills, but should also be ready to build a stalemate, mate the king, or simply play a couple of strong moves that stop the pawn.   Good luck!} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
