[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "016.002"]
[White "Introduction - The Mighty Queen"]
[Black "Part II - Chapter 4. The Mighty Queen"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The queen is the most powerful piece on the chessboard and is capable of deciding the game at once. The aim of this chapter is to recognize its tactical opportunities and learn how to exploit them with simple but powerful manoeuvres in your own games.   Queen vs rook   We kick off with pure queen versus rook endings in Section 4.1, where the queen is not hampered by any pawns. These positions allow us, among other things, to practice one of the most powerful weapons for the queen: the double attack. This important tactical resource is harmless in queen endings, because the enemy queen would simply capture our queen first.   In Section 4.2, we will first switch to the defender's perspective to learn how the rook can defend itself, and then switch back to demonstrate what the queen can do to overcome this resistance.   Queen versus queen   Next, we move on to queen versus queen endings, with one or more pawns for both sides. We will either deploy the skewer or the discovered attack to win a queen
(Section 4.3)
and train exercises where we need to mate the king in three or in four moves
(Section 4.4)
. I will also show you how we are capable of calculating much longer lines than we would expect.   Section 4.5 features slightly more challenging mix-exercises, where we only know that we need to win the game and have to figure out for ourselves whether we can reach this goal by either mating the king, or by winning the queen. Just like in our games.   Defend to survive and defend to win   We finish this chapter with two sections in which we must defend ourselves against queen tactics.   In Section 4.6, we must defend to survive. This means that we have to spot our opponent's potential tactic and avoid a direct defeat.   In Section 4.7, we raise the level of complexity by solving exercises in which we must defend to win. This means that staying alive is not sufficient anymore; we must also avoid perpetual checks to win the game.   All in all, we will have the following table of contents:   4.1 Catching the rook  4.2 Defending with rook against queen  4.3 Winning the queen   4.4 Mating the king   4.5 Winning the game  4.6 Defending to survive  4.7 Defending to win} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "017.002"]
[White "Introduction - The Mighty Queen - Catching the rook"]
[Black "4.1 The Mighty Queen - Catching the Rook"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The pure-queen-against rook ending is my favourite training environment for demonstrating the enormous potential of a queen. The absence of pawns enables the queen to travel freely across the board, and the fact that we play against a rook means that we are not limited to the skewer, but can also rely on the double attack as the weapon of our choice. This tactic would be harmless against a queen, because that piece would always capture our queen first when we attack it.   Capture the rook in three moves    The aim of this section is to sharpen our targeting and chasing skills by solving exercises in which we must capture the rook
(or mate the king)
within three moves. The key is to find those precise moves that place both our queen
(targeting)
and the enemy king
(chasing)
on the desired squares. With tempo! Not an easy task in an open position with many possible moves to consider.   To give you a head start, I have prepared four positions as examples that teach you powerful techniques you can use when solving the exercises. These techniques are:  1. Targeting   2. Chasing   3. The diagonal check   4. Exploiting the king at the rim} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "017.003"]
[White "1. Targeting"]
[Black "4.1 The Mighty Queen - Catching the Rook"]
[FEN "8/8/1Q6/1K1k4/8/8/r7/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{Our job in this position is to capture the rook in three moves.   If we want to find the best move, we should first notice that the enemy king and rook are already perfectly placed for a tactic. They are not just on a square of the same colour, but even on the same diagonal! A queen on b3 would, for instance, cause a double attack and one on f7, or g8, would win the rook with a skewer.   Based on this information, we should try to make the targeting technique work. We must find a way to move our queen to one of the desired squares with tempo—without allowing the king to move to a safe square.} 1. Qc5+ {[%cal Gc5d5,Rd5e6,Rd5e4][%csl Rd5]This is the right move! The black king is able to leave the diagonal, but has no other choice than to stay on the same colour and fall victim to our tactic on the next move.
1.Qc6+
is an instructive mistake as the black king can now move to a different colour with
1...Ke5
and escape from our tactics for the moment.} Ke4 {1...Ke6
runs into a similar double attack after
2.Qc4+
Ke5
3.Qxa2
.} 2. Qc4+ {[%cal Gc4e4,Gc4a2][%csl Re4,Ra2]} Ke5 {} 3. Qxa2 {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "017.004"]
[White "2. Chasing"]
[Black "4.1 The Mighty Queen - Catching the Rook"]
[FEN "5Q2/8/8/1K1k4/8/8/8/r7 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{We again have to capture the rook in three moves.   Note that this time, the king is on a light square, whereas the rook is on a dark one. With our queen already decently placed, we will now start with the chasing technique to force the king to a different colour.} 1. Qf5+ {[%cal Gf5d5,Rd5d6,Rd5d4][%csl Rd5]After this move, Black has the ugly choice between losing either with a double attack or a skewer.} Kd4 {This move runs into a skewer,
while
1...Kd6
2.Qf6+
would be the double attack.} 2. Qf6+ {[%cal Gf6a1][%csl Ra1,Rd4]} Kd5 {} 3. Qxa1 {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "017.005"]
[White "3. The diagonal check"]
[Black "4.1 The Mighty Queen - Catching the Rook"]
[FEN "8/2k5/8/1K4Q1/8/8/8/5r2 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The previous two examples taught us that it can be very effective to force the enemy piece to squares of the same colour. I will now demonstrate a special technique that we can use to reach this goal.  Our queen is very actively placed and looking for a way to attack the black king and rook along a diagonal. We will use the diagonal check to drive the black king to a different colour. The same colour as the one the rook is standing on.} 1. Qg3+ {[%cal Gg3b8][%csl Rc7,Rb8,Rd6]Checking the king along the diagonal has the advantage that most dark squares are being covered by the queen. Note how we attack the king and control both b8 and d6 on the same diagonal. Every possible light square, as well as the squares on the d-file, now run into a direct double attack, which means that the rook will be lost in two moves.} Kc8 {1...Kd8
was the only dark square available, but unfortunately for Black, is also losing due to
2.Qd3+
Ke8
3.Qxf1
.
1...Kb7
is another light square and leads to the same result after
2.Qg2+
Kc7
3.Qxf1
.} 2. Qh3+ {[%cal Gh3f1,Gh3c8][%csl Rf1,Rc8]The double attack along two diagonals!} Kc7 {} 3. Qxf1 {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "017.006"]
[White "4. The king at the rim"]
[Black "4.1 The Mighty Queen - Catching the Rook"]
[FEN "4k3/8/8/3Q4/8/8/5r2/K7 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{When it is not possible to chase the enemy king to the same colour as the rook, we must combine a diagonal attack on the rook with a horizontal
(or vertical)
attack on the king instead. In this case, using the edges of the board can be a very powerful technique.   We must still capture the rook in three moves. Our first task in this position is to list potential double attacks and make one of them work. Checking the king along the e-file could be a nice outcome, but manoeuvring your queen to e3
(or e1)
with tempo seems quite challenging. Therefore, we need to focus on attacking the king along the seventh rank, which brings us to the following move.} 1. Qa8+ {[%cal Ga8h8,Re8d7,Re8e7,Re8f7][%csl Re8]The king is standing on the edge of the board and can therefore be chased to the seventh rank with great ease.} Kd7 {} 2. Qa7+ {[%cal Ga7d7,Ga7f2][%csl Rf2,Rd7]Note how we successfully combined the chasing with the targeting technique. We not only chased the king to the seventh rank, but also moved our own queen to a dream square with tempo to create a double attack.} Kd6 {} 3. Qxf2 {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "017.007"]
[White "Conclusion - The Mighty Queen - Catching the rook"]
[Black "4.1 The Mighty Queen - Catching the Rook"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{This section taught us that if we want to capture the rook, the double attack and the skewer will be the most likely weapons of our choice. We practiced the ever-important chasing and targeting technique and learned that we can use diagonal checks to force the king to a different colour and use the edge of the board to prepare a deadly double attack.   It is now time to use these techniques to solve the exercises. Remember that the engines will show us many moves to win the game in these positions, but our task is to find the single route that enables us to capture the rook (or mate the king) in three moves.  Good luck!} 1. -- {} 


