[Result "*"]

{One of the most common defensive resources in drawish pawnless endgames is the 50-move rule. However, the queen versus rook ending is not drawish at all. Beating an engine would be a serious challenge as it knows exactly when and where to move its rook, but a human being does not have these calculation capabilities. This is why the games usually don't last until fifty moves after the last capture has taken place.   However, this does not mean that the rook always loses. On the contrary! There is a serious defensive resource that can be highly effective in a practical game. The stalemate trick! This will be our topic in this section. I will show you two examples in which we have to spot the stalemate pattern and create a crazy rook.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "018.003"]
[White "1. The Crazy Rook"]
[Black "4.2 The Mighty Queen - Defending with Rook versus Queen"]
[FEN "6RK/5q2/8/7k/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{We start with the most straightforward stalemate defence we can get.  The black queen already keeps our king caged, which means we only have to get rid of our rook to secure a draw.} 1. Rg5+ {[%cal Gf7g8,Gf7h7,Gh5g5][%csl Rg8,Rg7,Rh7,Gh8,Rg5]} Kh4 {} 2. Rg4+ {[%cal Gg4h4,Gf7h7,Gf7g8][%csl Rh4,Rh8]We should avoid
2.Rh5+
because
2...Qxh5+
would free our king and win for Black.} Kh5 {The black king has nothing better than to return.
After
2...Kh3
, we can simply keep the king trapped on the h-file with
3.Rg3+
.} 3. Rg5+ {[%cal Gg5h5,Gf7h7,Gf7g8][%csl Rh5,Rh8]It makes sense to return to our initial position,
although
3.Rh4+
Kg6
4.Rg4+
is also a draw.} Kh6 {} 4. Rg6+ {[%cal Rf7g6,Rg6g8,Rg6h7][%csl Gh8]This is a nice picture. Black can capture our rook in two different ways. They both lead to stalemate.} Qxg6 {[%cal Gg6g8,Gg6h7][%csl Rh8]4...Kxg6
is also a draw.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "018.004"]
[White "2. An important drawing mechanism"]
[Black "4.2 The Mighty Queen - Defending with Rook versus Queen"]
[FEN "5K2/R7/4q3/8/6k1/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The second example is less obvious and, therefore, a very frequent guest in practical games. Make sure you remember it well, as it may be your only chance to save the game or spoil the win, depending on which side you are on.   This stalemate trick needs three crucial ingredients to make it work. The first, and foremost one is the fact that we are to move!} 1. Rg7+ {[%cal Gg7g4,Gg7e7,Gg7h7][%csl Rg4,Gg7]The only ways in which we can restrain the powerful queen are to either attack the king or pin the queen. Any other attempt will enable the opponent to take over the initiative and change the position in their favour.} Kf5 {1...Kh5
2.Rh7+
Kg6
leads to the main line.} 2. Rf7+ {[%cal Gf7f5,Gf7e7,Gf7h7][%csl Rf5]The second ingredient of the drawing mechanism is the poor location of the queen. On e6, it not only hampers our king in its movement, it also prevents its own king from crossing the e-file. This means that it will be trapped on the f-, g-, and h-files forever.} Kg6 {This is the only serious try
as
2...Ke5
enables us to pin the queen with
3.Re7!
.} 3. Rg7+ {[%cal Gg7g6,Gg7e7,Gg7h7][%csl Rg6]} Kh6 {} 4. Rh7+ {[%cal Gh6h7,Ge6g8,Ge6e8,Gh7g7][%csl Rf8]This rook sacrifice is an important part of our plan. Capturing it leads to a stalemate, which means that the king must retreat and will fall victim to an endless series of checks.   Note that if the move 4...Ki6 were allowed, it would spoil all the fun. This makes the location of our king the third important ingredient of this drawing mechanism. It would not work if we mirror the position and place our king on d8. In that case, the black king could simply approach via the a-file and win.} Kg5 {The only serious try
as both
4...Kxh7
and
4...Kg6
5.Rh6+
Kxh6
lead to a stalemate.} 5. Rg7+ {[%cal Gg7g5,Gg7e7,Gg7h7][%csl Rg5]} Kf6 {This is the final position that you should remember. The outcome should not come as a surprise.} 6. Rg6+ {[%cal Gg6e6][%csl Rf6,Re6]} Kxg6 {[%cal Ge6e8,Ge6g8,Gg6g7][%csl Rf8]Stalemate!} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "018.005"]
[White "Conclusion - The Mighty Queen - Defending with Rook versus Queen"]
[Black "4.2 The Mighty Queen - Defending with Rook versus Queen"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{Always be alert for stalemate traps when you defend with the rook against a queen! This can be quite simple when we already have a crazy rook in place, but make sure you recognize the more complex opportunities against a poorly placed queen as well.   Solving the defensive exercises will help you in this process. You can start with six positions in which you must secure the draw as a defender. Next, we will switch perspectives and pick the right move for the attacker. The aim is to stay calm and make sure you don't let them trick you!   Good luck!} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "018.006"]
[White "Exercise 187 - The rook defends"]
[Black "4.2 The Mighty Queen - Defending with Rook versus Queen"]
[FEN "1R5K/5q2/8/6k1/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 3"]
[Result "*"]

3. Rg8+ {The key was to find the right check for our crazy rook.
3.Rb5+
Kf4!
4.Rb4+
Kf3
wins for Black as the queen controls both b3 and f4.} Kf4 {3...Kf6
4.Rg6+!
Kf5
5.Rg5+
also leads to a perpetual check along the g-file.} 4. Rg4+ {} Ke5 {} 5. Rg5+ {} Ke6 {} 6. Rg6+ {} Qxg6 {Gurgenidze (1999).} 


