4. g8=Q+ {I have added an extra pawn to the exercise to slightly increase the level of complexity. The pawn wont last long, but gives us a crazy rook.} Kxg8 {} 5. Rg4+ {Note that we have transposed to a mirrored version of Kuzmichev (1995). In my training practice, I have discovered that many students struggle to solve positions in which the rook defends along a rank instead of along a file. This is probably because we are trained to sit behind the board instead of next to it!} Kf8 {5...Qxg4
is a stalemate.
5...Kf7
6.Rg7+
Kf6
7.Rf7+
.} 6. Rg8+ {} Kxg8 {Whitworth (1974).
6...Kf7
7.Rf8+
