[Result "*"]

{Our aim in this first example is to chase the enemy king to the same rank, file or diagonal as its queen in order to execute the skewer. In this example, we are going for the a1-h8 diagonal.} 1. Qb7+ {[%cal Gb7c6,Rc6d6][%csl Rc6]This move keeps the king away from b5 and d5. It is important to select the right squares for the queen to give check and not allow the black king to reach the safe c4-square.
1.Qc7+
would not lead to the desired result after
1...Kb5
2.Qb7+
Kc4
.} Kd6 {} 2. Qd7+ {[%cal Gd7d6,Rd6e5][%csl Rd6]Again, the only one! The black king must be chased to e5.
2.Qc7+
is another way to push the black king to the a1-h8 diagonal, but after
2...Ke6
3.Qd7+
Kf6
it becomes clear that White is unable to get the queen to h8 in order to execute the skewer.} Ke5 {} 3. Qg7+ {[%cal Gg7a1][%csl Ra1,Re5]Here it is: the skewer !} Kd5 {} 4. Qxa1 {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
