{Before we move on to the exercises, I want to remind you of a rule that I stated in 1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Beginners:   The king can defend its queen after a check over a file (or rank), when there is only one empty square between the two pieces. However, this rule does not apply when the defender has to deal with a diagonal skewer.   Let me show you a simple example to refresh your memory.   A closer look at this position reveals that we can either force the black king to d3 or f3 to prepare a skewer. However, only one of them wins!} 1. Qh7+ {[%cal Gh7e4,Re4f3,Re4d4][%csl Re4]The right move. We must prepare a diagonal skewer.
1.Qe5+
is an instructive mistake due to
1...Kd3!
. With only one empty square between the king and queen, Black can defend against our vertical skewer after
2.Qd5+
Ke2
or
2...Kc2
.} Kf3 {1…
Kd4
2.Qd7+
and
1…
Kf4
2.Qf5#
als win for White.} 2. Qh5+ {[%cal Gh5d1][%csl Rd1,Rf3]Again, there is only one empty square between the king and queen, but Black is defenceless because the check is along the diagonal.} Kxf2 {} 3. Qxd1 {White wins.} 


