{Now that we have trained all the skills we need to win the rook and queen, it is time to focus on the enemy king. In this section, we will learn to mate the king in three and in four moves. In principle, all mating combinations, regardless of the number of moves deep, require the same two steps that we used for solving a mate in two in the first volume of this series. We have to find a mating pattern and then make sure we get it on the board. The amount of preparatory moves is the only element that distinguishes a mate in two from a mate in many more moves.   Mate in many moves   Solving a mate in four might sound scary at first, as you would expect that the mating exercises become significantly harder to solve when the number of moves increases. This is indeed often the case, but it is important to realize that the level of complexity also depends on two different variables:   1. Is the mating pattern obvious or hidden?   2. Are we dealing with a forcing line, or do we have to consider many alternatives for both players along the way?   I will present you with three different examples to elaborate on this subject, before we move on to solving exercises.   In the first one, we need to find a mate in eight, but we will discover that this does not require too much effort as the mating pattern is obvious and the moves are all forced.   Next, we will examine a position with a hidden mating pattern, where our path towards the mate is shorter, but also harder to spot.   Our last example features a type of solution that is easily overlooked in practice: the The silent move.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "020.003"]
[White "1. Mate in many moves"]
[Black "4.4 The Mighty Queen - Mating the King"]
[FEN "7k/1p5p/4K1p1/5pP1/6q1/8/8/7Q w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{Here is an example of a mate in eight
(!)
that would hopefully cause very little effort during a practical game.   The mating pattern is quite obvious in this position as positioning our queen on f8 would already be mate. The only step we need to take is to get our queen from h1 to f8 with tempo. You already know which tool we have to bring: targeting!} 1. Qa1+ {[%cal Ga1h8,Ga1a8][%csl Rh8,Ga8]} Kg8 {} 2. Qa8+ {[%cal Ga8g8,Ga8b7,Gb7c8,Gc8d7,Gd7e8,Ge8f7][%csl Rg8,Gf7]} Kg7 {} 3. Qxb7+ {[%cal Gb7g7,Gb7c8,Gc8d7,Gd7e8,Ge8f7][%csl Rg7,Gf7]Although there is a large emphasis on targeting in this exercise, we should not forget to make use of the chasing technique as well. We chase the black king back and forth to allow our queen to approach with tempo.} Kh8 {} 4. Qc8+ {[%cal Gc8h8,Gc8d7,Gd7e8,Ge8f7][%csl Rh8]} Kg7 {} 5. Qd7+ {[%cal Gd7g7,Gd7e8,Ge8f7][%csl Rg7,Gf7]} Kh8 {} 6. Qe8+ {[%cal Ge8h8,Ge8f7][%csl Rh8,Gf7]} Kg7 {The queen almost made it to the desired square. All that is left is a mate-in-two sequence to finish the job.} 7. Qf7+ {[%cal Gf7g7][%csl Rg7]} Kh8 {} 8. Qf8 {[%cal Gf8h8,Gf8g7][%csl Rh8]The level of this exercise was relatively low for three reasons:   1. The mating pattern was already visible;   2. The black king was unable to escape the mating net;   3. The white queen could approach with straight checks, without many alternatives.   The last two points resulted in a long and forcing line, without many alternatives to consider. Additionally, we were dealing with a mating pattern that we already knew and a targeting technique with which we were already familiar.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "020.004"]
[White "2. Hidden mating patterns"]
[Black "4.4 The Mighty Queen - Mating the King"]
[FEN "2Q5/6K1/1p6/1k6/1p6/1P6/P7/1q6 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The previous example demonstrated why solving mate-in-one and mate-in-two exercises
(and catching queens and rooks in a number of moves)
provides us with a very solid foundation for mastering longer
(and more complex)
lines. Let me show you another exercise to illustrate how a shorter mating combination can be more complex than a longer one and what we can do to overcome this.   If you have only little experience in solving mate-in-one or two exercises, you might find this one difficult. It is only a mate in three, but the mating pattern is still hidden, and it is unclear how it can be created by giving straight checks with our queen.  However, a trained eye will realize that we are dealing with a standard mate-in-two combination, if we were allowed to remove the b4-pawn. We need only one preparatory move to reach this goal:} 1. a4+ {[%cal Ga4b5,Rb4a3][%csl Rb5]This is the key move. The pawn opens up the fourth rank in order to allow the queen to setup a mating combination.} bxa3 {We have arrived at the standard mate-in-two combination. A pattern that can also be found in 1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Beginners.} 2. Qc4+ {[%cal Gc4b5,Rb5a5][%csl Rb5]This move chases the black king to the rim and targets the crucial a4-square.} Ka5 {} 3. Qa4 {[%cal Ga4a5][%csl Ra5]} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "020.005"]
[White "3. The silent move"]
[Black "4.4 The Mighty Queen - Mating the King"]
[FEN "Q7/8/4pkp1/p7/2p3K1/2P5/q1P5/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The power of forcing moves is a very important rule of thumb that can help you find the right continuation. However, you should always be careful with rules and be ready to ignore them when necessary. If you focus on forcing moves too much, you might miss its counterpart: the silent move! Without this important resource, you would not be able to solve this mate-in-three exercise.  The black queen is quite remote, which means that we can go after the black king undisturbed for the moment.} 1. Qf8+ {[%cal Gf8f6,Rf6e5][%csl Rf6]We start with chasing the enemy king to the centre.} Ke5 {} 2. Qd8 {[%cal Gd8d4,Re5d6,Re5d5,Re5d4][%csl Gd4]Next, we make sure the king cannot escape! More important, this pretty silent move prepares a deadly and undefendable mate in one. Note how the black queen is still very remote and therefore unable to stop us.} Qxc2 {} 3. Qd4 {[%cal Gd4f6,Gd4d6,Gd4f4][%csl Re5]} 


