{I hope you have already started to appreciate the trickiness of a queen on an almost empty board. If you were able to solve the exercises in the previous sections, you can be confident that you will spot most of the tactics that may arise in queen endings in your own game.   However, I must warn you: finding a tactic for yourself is often much easier than foreseeing one for your opponent. Especially when the motif is caused by the move that you are about to make and is therefore not visible yet, and especially when you run short on time.   The following game, played by one of the world's strongest blitz players, can serve as a nice example.  This is an online Rapid game that was played in 2022 between the Grandmasters Dubov and Kovalev. With two extra pawns, Dubov is on his way to decide the game in his favour. Any legal move would give him a winning position. Except for...} 77. Kh6 {This unfortunate move creates a mate-in-one exercise.} Qh7 {Mistakes like this can happen with little time on the clock. Even among top players. This example underlines the following conclusion:   Defending against a tactical combination is much harder than finding one yourself!   For two reasons:   1. We have to visualize one extra move (our own move);   2. We are trained to solve exercises in which we win games. Defence is often neglected. Therefore, we find it much harder to find tactical resources for our opponent.   Checkmate a Grandmaster   This game was just one example among many in the Megadatabase where a highly skilled player misses, or causes, a deadly queen tactic. To illustrate this, I have selected six games in which a grandmaster goes wrong and present them to you as exercises.   Your task will be to spot the tactic that decided the game. Not only will this give you a few light exercises between all the hard work, but it’s also a delightful opportunity to checkmate a strong grandmaster!
 Good luck!} 


