[Result "*"]

{I hope you got the point and realize that we need to stay alert in tactical endings to prevent our opponent from surprising us. Another thing we can do is to solve exercises in which we are on the defending side. That is where this and the following section step in. You will be able to simulate situations in which you need to defend yourself against your opponent's threats.  Defend to survive   Your job in this section will be to defend to survive. This means that a draw can be a perfectly viable outcome. All you need to do is ensure you don't lose the game. Before we begin, I will provide an example to give you an idea of what to expect.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "022.010"]
[White "Pick the right square"]
[Black "4.6 The Mighty Queen - Defending to Survive"]
[FEN "5Q2/1p6/1Ppkppp1/5qp1/pP6/2P4P/P5P1/7K b - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{Our king is under attack in this position and must pick the right square to stay alive. The big question is whether we shall push it towards the centre or keep it between our own pawns.   This is not a simple decision to make, but fortunately, we have two useful techniques that we can rely on:   1. The power of the forcing moves   2. The elimination technique.   Since we have only three legal moves available, we can start with the most forcing ones. If a move doesn't work, we eliminate it and move on to the next one.   In this position, 1... Kd7 looks like the hardest one to assess, which means we save it for last in our calculation. Once we conclude that both other moves lead to a direct, forced mate, we eliminate them and, after a brief double check, we play} 1. Kd7 {and hope for the best.
These were the lines we had to calculate:
1...Kd5
2.Qc5+
Ke4
3.Qd4
1...Ke5
2.Qc5+
Kf4
3.Qf2+
Ke4
4.Qd4
.} 2. Qf7+ {Here we have a new decision to make, again with three legal moves.
2...Kc8
Qc7#
does not look good for us, which gives us the choice between going back or forward with the king.
2...Kd8
loses the b-pawn with tempo and enables the opponent to use the targeting technique to find the perfect spot for the queen with
3.Qc7+
Ke8
4.Qc8+
Kf7
5.Qxb7+
Kg8
6.Qb8+
Kg7
7.b7
.
The conclusion is therefore to move back to d6, after which White has nothing better than picking up the b-pawn and allowing a perpetual check.} Kd6 {} 3. Qxb7 {} Qf1+ {} 4. Kh2 {} Qf4+ {It is a perpetual and we have survived the game!} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "022.011"]
[White "Conclusion - The Mighty Queen - Defending to Survive"]
[Black "4.6 The Mighty Queen - Defending to Survive"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{Defending against a tactical combination is much harder than finding one yourself because it requires us to visualize one extra move, and defence is often neglected in chess training. This is why I have prepared numerous exercises in which we must find the only move that keeps the game level.   Your task will be to defend to survive. Make sure you have the power of the forcing moves and the elimination technique ready!   Good luck!} 1. -- {} 


