{In the second set of defensive exercises, called defend to win, it is no longer good enough to escape with a draw. You have to end the line with a winning position! This makes life more difficult because not only do you have to keep your king and queen safe, but you also need to be vigilant for perpetuals. I will provide one more example before we proceed to the actual exercises.  Our king is under attack in this position, and we only have three legal moves to choose from. Just like in the previous section, we can again make use of the forcing lines first and the elimination technique. With two differences.   First, we have to eliminate lines that lead to a draw as well, and second, we no longer have nothing to lose. This means you must double-check your final decision more carefully, as we might want to prefer the drawing line over a direct loss.} 1. Kh3 {The alternatives both lead to a quick mate:
1...Kg3
2.Qg6+
Kh3
3.Qg4
1...Kh5
2.Qf5+
Kh4
3.Qg4
.} 2. Qe6+ {} Kg2 {Again, the only move that keeps us alive, as
2...Kg3
3.Qg4
and
2...Kh4
3.Qg4
are mate.} 3. Qa2+ {} Kh1 {This time we wanted more than just staying alive! This move puts an end to all White's checks as
4.Qa1+
Qg1+
trades queens and wins for Black.
Although there is still a lot of work to do after
4.Qxa3
, we can look to the future with confidence, with two extra pawns and a relatively safe king.
Note that
3...Kg3
4.Qg8+
Kh3
5.Qg4
would lead to a familiar mate and Black does not make any progress after both
3...Kg1
4.Qb1+
Kg2
5.Qa2+
and
3...Kh3
4.Qe6+
Kg2
5.Qa2+
.  Exercises  It is time to move on to the exercises. The aim is to make the right king, queen, or pawn moves that keep your winning advantage.  Good luck!} 


