[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "024.002"]
[White "Introduction - The Reliable Rook"]
[Black "Part II - Chapter 5. The Reliable Rook"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The first principles that we learn as a beginning chess player are the golden rules. They tell us that we should put a pawn in the centre, develop our minor pieces, and bring our king into safety.   If we study these rules more closely, we can deduce that it is the light pieces that come into action first, and the rooks often follow at an appropriate distance. In many cases, they only arrive at the scene when other pieces have already been traded. This is why it is easy to understand that of all endgames, rook endgames have by far the largest share and are therefore very important to study.   Rook versus rook.   We start this chapter with endings in which both sides possess a single rook and a number of pawns. Section 5.1 teaches us powerful techniques to promote a pawn, where mating the king will be our aim in Section 5.2.   We increase the level of complexity in Section 5.3, as it invites us to solve winning the game exercises where we won't get any hints on how to achieve this goal, or how many moves it will take.   Section 5.4 teaches us various techniques to defend ourselves by either neutralizing our opponent’s threat, or by deploying various drawing resources like the stalemate trap.   Rook versus Minor Piece  We end this chapter with pawnless endings where the rook has to battle against a minor piece. These endings are a theoretical draw, but Sections 5.5 to 5.8 teach us that the rook versus knight and rook versus bishop endings might not always be as simple as we would think. They teach us important techniques to win against all odds with the rook, or to defend well with the minor piece.   In summary, we will have the following table of contents in this chapter:   5.1 Promoting a pawn   5.2 Mating the king    5.3 Winning the game   5.4 Saving the game   5.5 Catching the knight   5.6 Defending the knight   5.7 Catching the bishop   5.8 Defending the bishop} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "025.002"]
[White "Introduction- The Reliable Rook - Promoting your pawn"]
[Black "5.1 The Reliable Rook - Promoting your pawn"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The most common way to decide a rook ending in your favour is by promoting one of your pawns into a queen. Very often, we can only reach this goal by bringing over our king, and, as this is our most patient piece, you can imagine that this can be a rather slow and technical process. I believe that this is also the reason why many chess players label rook endings as slightly dull.   Tactical rook endings   The good news for us is that we can completely ignore this 'boring' part. Instead, we focus on tactical rook endgames in which our rook gives the pawn the decisive push. And deal with positions in which we skillfully neutralize the enemy pieces, after which our pawn can move on unhindered. I will give you three examples to illustrate these scenarios before we move on to the exercises.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "025.003"]
[White "1. The rook assists the pawn"]
[Black "5.1 The Reliable Rook - Promoting your pawn"]
[FEN "8/4k3/7R/P4p2/6p1/8/5PK1/4r3 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{In this first example, you can see that our king is still very remote and cannot really play a role in assisting our pawn. This is where we need our rook to step in!} 1. a6 {[%cal Ga6a8][%csl Ga8]We start with pushing our pawn as far as possible.} Ra1 {} 2. a7 {[%cal Ga7a8,Gh6h7,Gh7a7,Ga1a7][%csl Ga8,Re7,Ra7]This second pawn move is supported by a tactic that is very common in rook endings and therefore important to remember.} Kd7 {The king tries to join the defence, but it will be too late.
The point behind our last move was
2...Rxa7
3.Rh7+
Kf6
4.Rxa7
.} 3. Rh8 {[%cal Ga7a8,Ra1a7,Gh8h7,Gh7a7][%csl Ga8,Ra7,Rd7]The rook can safely move to the back rank as our pawn is still tactically defended. It is game over as we threaten to promote our pawn and we have the following skewer ready in case the rook decides to take it:} Rxa7 {} 4. Rh7+ {[%cal Gh7a7][%csl Ra7,Rd7]} Kd6 {} 5. Rxa7 {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "025.004"]
[White "2. Blocking the file"]
[Black "5.1 The Reliable Rook - Promoting your pawn"]
[FEN "8/4p3/p1p3P1/5p1k/R7/8/K1P4P/4r3 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{This second example has another key role for our rook. Only this time, it must sacrifice itself to block the open file, after which our pawn decides the game.   Our pawn is under attack and Black is also ready to catch it with ...Re1-g1. It is time for our rook to step in.} 1. Rg4 {[%cal Rh5g4,Gg6g8,Ge1g1,Gg1g8][%csl Gg8,Rg4]A fantastic move! Black can capture the rook in two different ways, but both options block the file and leave our pawn unopposed.} Kxg4 {1...fxg4
2.g7
is a very similar line, while ignoring the rook with
1...Kh6
loses to
2.g7!
.} 2. g7 {The pawn promotes and White should win the queen versus rook ending without too much trouble.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "025.005"]
[White "3. Luring the rook"]
[Black "5.1 The Reliable Rook - Promoting your pawn"]
[FEN "8/2p2p1p/P2p4/5P2/8/2k1r3/K1P5/7R w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{In this third and final example, we need to lure away the defending rook to reach our goal.   Black threatens to stop our pawn with ...Re3-e8-a8 and we cannot block the e-file, nor the back rank. Luckily, we have a different technique that we can use.} 1. Rh3 {[%cal Re3h3,Gh3h8,Ga6a8][%csl Rh7,Ga8]This move lures away the rook and secures a safe pathway for our pawn. Note that the rook is also pinned, which prevents it from ignoring our rook and moving to the back rank.} Rxh3 {} 2. a7 {[%cal Ga7a8,Gh3h8][%csl Ga8,Rh7]Black cannot stop our pawn and we win the game.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
