{The quickest way to decide the game is, of course, to mate the enemy king. A sudden mate does not occur very often in rook endings, but I will show you two techniques that we can rely on in case the opportunity arises.   In the first example, we use the blocking technique to force the enemy rook to obstruct its own king, after which we deliver the mate.   In the second example, we can suddenly cut off the king along a rank to close the mating net.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "026.003"]
[White "1. Blocking a square"]
[Black "5.2 The Reliable Rook - Mating the King"]
[FEN "6R1/8/4pK2/7k/7r/8/6P1/8 w - - 0 4"]
[Result "*"]

{This first example is the final part of a study composed by Maricsics in 2001.   The black king is already hampered in its movement and we are going to create a blockade on g4 to seal its fate.} 4. g4+ {[%cal Rh4g4,Gg8h8][%csl Rg4]This is the key move. All that is left for Black is to choose how the king will be mated.} Rxg4 {This leads to the most instructive mate.
4...Kh6
5.Rh8
would be the other option.} 5. Rh8 {[%cal Gh8h5,Rh5g4][%csl Gh5,Rg4]} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "026.004"]
[White "2. Cutting off the king"]
