{In this section, we are going to solve exercises in which we only know that we need to win the game. This makes our job slightly more complex because we won't know whether we must promote a pawn, win a rook, or mate the king in a certain number of moves.   So far, we’ve already seen plenty of examples of promoting pawns and checkmating the king. Therefore, before we move on to the exercises, I’ll now take the opportunity to show you how you might win a rook in a rook versus rook endgame.  This study was composed by Blandford in 1952. The elements we need to spot here are Black's unprotected rook and vulnerable king.} 5. Kd6 {[%cal Gh5h4,Gh4h3,Gh4d4,Gh3a3][%csl Rd4,Ra3,Rd3]It is surprisingly hard for Black to defend against this mate-in-one threat. The only way out is moving the king to the third rank.} Kxd3 {The mate threat has been averted for the moment, but with both black pieces on the same rank, we should not have any difficulty in picking the right tool for deciding the game.
Both
5...Rxd3
6.Rh4
and
5...a5
6.Rd5
lead to a direct mate.} 6. Rh3+ {[%cal Gh3a3][%csl Ra3,Rd3]The skewer!} Kd4 {} 7. Rxa3 {Exercises   It is now time to solve lots of win the game exercises. Pay close attention to unprotected rooks, vulnerable kings, and impatient passed pawns as they might guide you towards the right moves.   Good luck!} 


