{It is now time to switch to defensive mode again and make sure we save the game in a rook versus rook ending. I have created many exercises in which we either have to play a precise move to neutralize our opponent's threat, or take more drastic measures, such as the stalemate trap or a perpetual check.   The crazy rook   Unsurprisingly, there will be a prominent role for crazy rooks in this chapter. This means we can use our skills from the queen versus rook section! To refresh your memory, I will present you with one more example of this powerful drawing resource.  This brilliant study was composed by Rinck in 1909. It consists of two parts. First, we need to create a stalemate pattern for our king, which turns our rook crazy. The second step is to get rid of this rook and secure the draw.} 1. Re5+ {[%cal Ge5a5,Ra5b4,Gb7a6][%csl Ra5]We must push back the black king to clear the a6-square for our king. This will turn out to be an essential part of our plan.
1.fxg7
looks tempting, but results in a losing rook ending after
1...Rd7+
2.Kc6
Rxg7
.} Kb4 {1...Ka4
2.fxg7
is very similar to the main line.} 2. fxg7 {[%cal Gg7g8][%csl Rg8]This is the right moment for capturing the pawn.} Rd7+ {2...Rd8
is very slow and leads to a drawn rook ending after
3.Kxa7
Rd7+
4.Kb6
Rxg7
5.Rg5
Kc4
6.Kc6
Kd4
7.Kd6
Ke4
8.Ke6
Kf4
9.Kf6
.} 3. Ka6 {[%cal Gd7a7,Gg7g8][%csl Ra6,Gg8]} Rxg7 {Our king is unable to move, which means the stalemate pattern is in place. It is now time for our crazy rook to step in.} 4. Re7 {[%cal Rg7e7][%csl Ra6]} Rg8 {The best attempt, as
4...Rxe7
would immediately result in a stalemate.} 5. Rg7 {[%cal Rg8g7,Gg7a7][%csl Ra6,Ga7]We can only sacrifice our rook if it lures the black rook to the seventh rank.
5.Re8
would be a serious mistake because
5...Rxe8
is not a stalemate!} Rxg7 {Mission accomplished!
Note that
5...Rd8
leads to a simple draw after
6.Rxg6
. However, we should not get too excited and must avoid
6.Rd7
as
6...Rb8!
7.Rg7
Rb6+
8.Kxa7
Rd6
wins for Black.
 Conclusion  This example taught us once again that the crazy rook can be a very powerful weapon to save the game. It also reminded us that we should stay focused until the very end and make sure we don't get overexcited. Especially in our own games where we do not know whether brilliancy is required to win the game, or just a plain but strong move.   Exercises   To simulate our game setting as much as possible, I have created a mix of exercises where both highly creative and simple and strong moves are required.   Good luck!} 


