{It is now time to move on to the rook versus knight ending, without any pawns on the board. This ending is known as a theoretical draw, but it surprised me how easy it is to find practical examples in which the rook side prevails. In some cases, because of the unfortunate positioning of the defending pieces at the start, and also because finding the right defensive moves turned out to be much harder than you would expect.   I am convinced that grinding this ending until the very end in your games will get you many unexpected bonus points especially when you have the most important attacking techniques in your toolbox.   Three examples   In this section, I will show you three examples that should help you to play this ending with more confidence. First, we invite Fabiano Caruana to demonstrate the drawing mechanism for the knight.   Next, we discuss two circumstances under which the defending knight might end up in trouble. These are positions where the knight is close to the edge of the board, or situations where it gets disconnected from the king.   We end this section with exercises where we must try to mate the king or catch the knight, before turning over to the knight side in Section 5.6.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "029.003"]
[White "1. The drawing procedure"]
[Black "5.5 The Reliable Rook - Catching the Knight"]
[FEN "4K3/r4P2/4k3/8/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 86"]
[Result "*"]

{Many of the rook against knight endings result from a rook against pawn ending. This was also the case in the following 2023 blitz game that was played in Zagreb between Fabiano Caruana
(2782)
and Ivan Saric
(2657)
.} 86. f8=N+ {[%cal Gf8e6,Ga7a8,Ga8e8][%csl Re6,Re8]Promoting the pawn into a knight with check was forced, as a back rank mate was threatened.} Kd6 {This is often the best try for the attacker as
86...Kf6
87.Nd7+
Ke6
88.Nf8+
is just a repetition of the moves.} 87. Ng6 {[%cal Gg6f8][%csl Gf8,Ge8]This move is easy to find because everything else loses on the spot. It is important to know that sending the knight away from the king for one or two moves is not a serious crime in this ending, but make sure it can always return quickly, as otherwise your opponent might find a way to separate them for good.} Rg7 {} 88. Nf8 {[%csl Gf8,Ge8]} Re7+ {} 89. Kd8 {} Re4 {} 90. Ng6 {[%cal Gg6f8][%csl Gf8]Caruana repeats the same manoeuvre, although
90.Nd7
would also be fine for him.} Rg4 {} 91. Nf8 {[%cal Gd8e8][%csl Ge8,Gf8]} Rg7 {} 92. Ke8 {[%csl Ge8,Gf8]} Re7+ {} 93. Kd8 {} Ra7 {} 94. Ke8 {[%csl Ge8,Gf8]We have returned to the initial position, which means that Black did not make any progress. The fact that it is now Black to move does not make any difference. Saric will play on for a few more moves before the draw is agreed.} Rb7 {} 95. Ng6 {[%cal Gg6f8][%csl Gf8,Ge8]} Ke6 {} 96. Nf8+ {[%csl Ge8,Gf8]} Kf6 {} 97. Nd7+ {[%cal Gd7f8][%csl Ge8,Gf8]} Kf5 {} 98. Ke7 {[%csl Ge7,Gd7]} Ra7 {} 99. Kd6 {[%csl Gd7,Gd6]} Rxd7+ {} 100. Kxd7 {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "029.004"]
[White "2. The unfortunate knight"]
[Black "5.5 The Reliable Rook - Catching the Knight"]
[FEN "6K1/1r5P/6k1/8/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 81"]
[Result "*"]

{This position looks a lot like the one Caruana played. Still, we will soon discover that the evaluation is entirely different when we must play with a rook's pawn.} 81. h8=N+ {[%cal Gb7b8,Gb8g8,Gh8g6][%csl Rg8,Rg6]} Kf6 {[%cal Gf6g6,Gf6f7,Gb7f7][%csl Rh8,Rf7,Rg6]It turns out that the corner is a very unfortunate place for the knight! We can safely resign here.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "029.005"]
[White "3. Disconnecting the king and knight"]
[Black "5.5 The Reliable Rook - Catching the Knight"]
[FEN "8/2r5/8/8/3N4/3k4/1K6/8 w - - 0 97"]
[Result "*"]

{In this last example, I will demonstrate a very powerful technique for deciding the rook against knight ending in your favour. We will try to separate the knight from the king, make sure it cannot return, and eventually catch it. Be aware that, unlike in the previous example, a little help from your opponent is required.  This position was reached in Hoang Van Ngoc-Cao Sang, Vietnam teams 2013. White was defending well in this ending, but got himself into serious trouble with the next move.} 97. Nf3 {Bringing the knight too far from the king is often tricky as one of the main goals for the rook is to separate the knight from the king. The remainder of the game teaches us why.
97.Nb3
was required to keep the knight close to the king.} Re7 {Not the only move, but a strong one. Very often, we want our king and rook to control the files right next to the knight to prevent it from returning home.} 98. Kc1 {} Re3 {This time, Black had to be precise!
The knight would return safely to the king, after, for instance,
98...Re2
99.Kd1!
Rf2
100.Ne1+
.} 99. Ng5 {} Re1+ {Black has many winning alternatives during the next couple of moves, but his play is nevertheless instructive.} 100. Kb2 {} Re2+ {} 101. Kb3 {} Kd4 {} 102. Nf3+ {} Ke4 {The king pushes back the knight towards the g-file.} 103. Ng5+ {} Kf4 {} 104. Nf7 {} Rd2 {This was another critical move!
104...Re6
105.Kc4!
, followed by 106.Kd5 and Nf7-d6 would reunite the white pieces and spoil the win for Black.} 105. Nh6 {} Rd7 {} 106. Kc4 {} Kg5 {White resigned because he loses the knight after} 107. Ng8 {} Rg7 {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "029.006"]
[White "Conclusion - The Reliable Rook - Catching the Knight"]
[Black "5.5 The Reliable Rook - Catching the Knight"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{This section taught us that it can be very rewarding to play on until the very end in a rook against knight ending, even though it is a theoretical draw.   To maximize your chances, I have created a set of realistic exercises in which you can decide the game at once — in some cases by mating the king, in others by forcing the knight closer to the edge, or by making sure you disconnect the knight from the king.   Good luck!} 1. -- {} 


