{The previous section already gave us a lot of information on how we should defend ourselves with a knight against a rook. First and foremost, we had a masterclass from Caruana, showing the most important drawing mechanisms.  On top of that, we could practice powerful techniques for the attacker, like disconnecting the king and knight and pushing the knight to the edge.   With these lessons in mind, we should already be able to solve the defending exercises that I have created for you in this section. However, before we get started, there is a little experiment that I would like to share with you.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "030.003"]
[White "Picking the right square - Part 1"]
[Black "5.6 The Reliable Rook - Defending the Knight"]
[FEN "6R1/8/8/8/8/7K/8/5n1k b - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{I have created a position in which our king is unable to move and we need to find the right square for our knight. The question is:   What would you play?} 1. Nd2 {This is the only move that keeps the game level!
It was very important to avoid
1...Ne3
2.Kg3!
, which leads to a mirrored version of one of the exercises in the previous section. It is game over after
2...Nf1+
2...Kg1
3.Kf3+
3.Kf2
Nh2
4.Rg1
.
Note that
1...Nh2
also does not work as it leads to the exact same mate after
2.Kg3!
Kg1
2...Nf1
3.Kf2!
Nh2
4.Rg1#
3.Rg7!
Nf1
4.Kf3+
Kh1
5.Kf2
Nh2
6.Rg1#} 2. Rf8 {Both
2.Rd8
Nf1
and
2.Kg3
Kg1!
shouldn't impress us too much.} Kg1 {Black brings the knight to f1 on the next move and is doing fine.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "030.004"]
[White "Picking the right square - Part 2"]
[Black "5.6 The Reliable Rook - Defending the Knight"]
[FEN "8/8/8/8/8/7n/R7/5K1k b - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{I will now show you a second position in which we need to place our knight on the best square possible.  What would you play?} 1. Ng5 {You probably found this move without too much trouble. And noted that we were solving the exact same position again, only with the board rotated. The interesting thing is that I discovered that many students found this one considerably harder than the previous one and sometimes go for
1...Nf4
. Most likely because the knight feels less remote there, and probably also because we tend to automate patterns where we play from the bottom to the top, and therefore feel disoriented in pawnless endings when we suddenly need to play horizontally. We already know the problem with this move:
2.Kf2
Nh3+
3.Kg3
Ng1
4.Rh2
.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "030.005"]
[White "Conclusion - The Reliable Rook - Defending the Knight"]
[Black "5.6 The Reliable Rook - Defending the Knight"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{What lesson can we draw from our two similar exercises with different board orientations? Be very careful with blindly following your pattern recognition! It might play tricks on you, so make sure you base your final decision on precise calculation!   Switching the board orientation   We can also use this knowledge as an attacker in various pawnless endings. When your opponent defends well in the bottom-to-the-top orientation, try switching to the horizontal mode in an attempt to create confusion.   Exercises   It is now time for the exercises. The aim is to calculate well and find the right move. I have deliberately mixed rather simple and more complex moves to simulate a game setting as much as possible.   Good luck!} 1. -- {} 


