{The bishop is more predictable than a knight, which makes the pawnless rook against bishop ending much easier to handle.   This section demonstrates why placing the king in the right corner is the most important defending technique and teaches us that the attacker needs to lock up the king in the wrong corner to decide the game.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "031.003"]
[White "1. King in right corner"]
[Black "5.7 The Reliable Rook - Catching the Bishop"]
[FEN "7k/5K2/8/8/6R1/3b4/8/8 b - - 0 88"]
[Result "*"]

{The most important defensive rule we must remember in the bishop against rook ending is to put the king in the corner on a colour that is opposite to the bishop.   Black achieved their goal in this position. All that is left is simply moving the bishop up and down the diagonal and relying on stalemate whenever possible. For the rest, there are only two 'tactics' that are useful to know and that I will show you.} 88. Bh7 {[%csl Gh8,Gh7]} 89. Rg1 {It is already quite hard to make progress here as a move like
89.Rh4
is always stalemate.} Bc2 {[%cal Gc2h7][%csl Gh8,Gh7]As long as we simply move our bishop up and down the diagonal, we should be fine. There are only two 'tactics' we need to be aware of with the king in the right corner.} 90. Rg4 {90.Rg8+
Kh7
91.Rg2
This is the first thing White could try. Here, we should remember that the simple check
91...Bb3+!
is good enough. We are totally fine after, for instance,
92.Kf8
Bc4
93.Rh2+
Kg6
. Note that we control the g8-square, which means that our king can return to the corner as soon as the rook leaves the h-file. Another option is simply to cross the board all the way to a1.} Bh7 {[%csl Gh7,Gh8]We stick to our plan and move our bishop up and down the diagonal. If we do this consistently, we will never run into the second 'trick' that I wanted to show you.
This one arises after the horrible
90...Bf5
. This is the best attempt to lose this ending. Our bishop cannot attack the enemy king from here, which means that White wins after
91.Rg8+!
Kh7
92.Rg5
.} 91. Rg1 {} Bc2 {[%cal Gc2h7][%csl Gh7,Gh8]} 92. Rg2 {} Bh7 {[%csl Gh7,Gh8]I think we can safely stop here, or end the game peacefully after} 93. Rh2 {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "031.004"]
[White "2. King in the wrong corner"]
[Black "5.7 The Reliable Rook - Catching the Bishop"]
[FEN "6k1/5R2/6K1/8/1b6/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The situation becomes less peaceful for the defender when the king gets locked up in the wrong corner.   This is the position that we are aiming at as an attacker. Suddenly, all kinds of tactics start to work in our favour!} 1. Rb7 {[%cal Gb7b4,Gb7b8,Gb8g8][%csl Rb4,Rg8]} Bd6 {Black can parry the first double attack by controlling the back rank with the bishop, but this defensive technique is not sustainable at all. It is game over after} 2. Rd7 {[%cal Gd7d6,Gd7d8,Gd8g8][%csl Gd8,Rd6,Rg8]} Bc5 {} 3. Rd8+ {[%cal Gd8g8][%csl Rg8]} Bf8 {} 4. Rb8 {[%cal Gb8f8,Rg8h8][%csl Rf8]} Kh8 {} 5. Rxf8 {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
