102. Rd8 {We must chase the bishop to an unfortunate square so we can use it later for locking up the king.
In Gagliardo-Velasquez, Merida 1997, White went for
102.Kf2
, after which Black equalized with
102...Kh3!
, but nevertheless lost the game.} Ba4 {102...Bg4
103.Kf2
loses on the spot and
102...Bb3
103.Kf2
locks up the king as
103...Kh3
loses a piece to
104.Rd3+!
.} 103. Kf2 {} Kh3 {The best attempt to leave the corner, but still not a very convincing one.} 104. Rd3+ {} Kh2 {} 105. Rd4 {The double attack on the bishop and the h4-square decides the game.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
