1. Kf7 {The black king is in the right corner in this position, but the bishop is definitely not on the right square!} Kh6 {1...Kh8
2.Rg8+
Kh7
3.Rg5
is a motif that we already witnessed in the Introduction.} 2. Kf6 {This is the point. Black must spend a move on relocating the bishop and loses.} Bd7 {} 3. Rd3 {This is the most thematic move, although the engine indicates that both
3.Rg5
and
3.Rg7
win as well.} Bg4 {The bishop must stay on the c8-h3 diagonal to avoid a direct mate.
3...Bc8
4.Rd8
Ba6
5.Rh8
.} 4. Rd2 {} Kh7 {4...Kh5
5.Rh2+
is another instructive position. The bishop is very poorly placed on g4.} 5. Rh2+ {} Kg8 {} 6. Rg2 {This pin decides the game.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "031.009"]
[White "Exercise 400 - Catching the bishop"]
[Black "5.7 The Reliable Rook - Catching the Bishop"]
[FEN "8/7k/6R1/5K2/5b2/8/8/8 w - - 0 96"]
[Result "*"]

96. Kf6 {The black king is already locked up in the corner, which means we only have to improve our king to decide the game.} Be5+ {} 97. Kf7 {This is the only move that makes sense, but it is still interesting to point out that
97.Kf5
Bb2
would spoil everything because we can no longer keep the black king caged.} Kh8 {Black knew what was coming and decided to surrender politely.} 98. Rh6 {Kuijf-Böhm, Wijk aan Zee 1983.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "031.010"]
[White "Exercise 401 - Catching the bishop"]
[Black "5.7 The Reliable Rook - Catching the Bishop"]
[FEN "8/8/8/5k2/3K4/8/7R/1b6 w - - 0 70"]
[Result "*"]

70. Rb2 {This is what we call an unfortunate location for the bishop. In Kalugin-Tikhonov, Orel 1997, Black resigned in view of} Be4 {} 71. Rf2+ {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "031.011"]
[White "Exercise 402 - Catching the bishop"]
[Black "5.7 The Reliable Rook - Catching the Bishop"]
[FEN "8/k7/1bK1R3/8/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 70"]
[Result "*"]

70. Re7+ {The king was in the right corner, but the bishop was not ready to complete the defensive set-up. It is game over.} Ka6 {} 71. Re2 {This is just one of many rook moves that decide the game. Black resigned in Dobrich-Kegel, Toronto 1967.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "032.002"]
[White "Introduction - The Reliable Rook - Defending the Bishop"]
[Black "5.8 The Reliable Rook - Defending the Bishop"]
[FEN "8/3Rbk2/8/4K3/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 66"]
[Result "*"]

{The previous section already taught us that when you are defending with a bishop against a rook, you should place your king in the right corner. This is the one where it stands on a different colour than your bishop. This is sometimes easier said than done because our opponent will, of course, try hard to prevent this and push our king into the wrong corner instead.   The following example could help you thwart your opponent's winning plan and secure a draw.  This position was reached in the game Tahbaz - Movahed, played in the Iranian Championships in 2023. It is an important position for the defender. We must take two important steps to keep our bishop safe and to make sure our king won't be locked up in the corner.} 66. Kf8 {[%cal Re5e6,Ge7h4]The first step is to pick the right square! In this case the one that prevents the enemy king from creating a mating net by moving to an opposite square.
66...Ke8
would be further away from the corner, but loses the bishop after
67.Ke6
Bg5
68.Rg7
Bh6
69.Rg8+
Bf8
70.Rh8
. We are in zugzwang and lose the bishop.} 67. Ke6 {} Bh4 {[%cal Gh4f6,Re6f6][%csl Gf6]The second step is to keep the f6-square under control! This prevents the opponent from locking up our king for good.
67...Ba3
was played in the game. Black resigned after
68.Rf7+
Kg8
69.Kf6
in view of, for instance,
69...Bd6
70.Rg7+
70.Kg6
also wins, but takes more time
Kh8
70...Kf8
71.Rd7
71.Kg6
.} 68. Rh7 {} Bg5 {[%cal Gg5f6][%csl Rf6]We stick to our plan. The f6-square is still under control and the hard work is almost over.} 69. Rf7+ {[%cal Gf8e8,Gf7g7,Gg7g8,Gg7g5][%csl Rg8,Rg5]} Kg8 {This is another critical move. It feels tricky to move the king towards the wrong corner, but we will be safe as long as we keep the f6-square under control.
69...Ke8
70.Rg7
is a trick that we already know and that we must avoid.} 70. Ra7 {} Kf8 {[%cal Gg5f6][%csl Rf6]White has run out of ideas and the game is about to end in a draw.
 Conclusion  Moving your king straight towards the right corner is not always possible in the bishop against rook ending. In that case, we should at least make sure that our king won't be trapped in the wrong corner.   This section taught us a two-step plan that should keep us safe. This plan consisted of picking the right square for the king, which is f8,  and controlling the opposite square on the sixth rank with the bishop, which is f6.   Exercises   There are six exercises waiting for you that require a critical move to stay in the game. They will help you to defend this ending with confidence in your future games.   Good luck!} 


