{With the queen and rook skills already in our pocket, it is now time to move on to the minor pieces, starting with the knight.   Promote your pawn  The aim in this chapter will be to promote our pawn. You will notice that the exercises share many similarities with the ones we solved in Chapter 2, when we discussed pawns versus knight and pawns versus bishop endings. Adding a knight to our camp adds more complexity to the positions, allowing us to build an extra layer on this solid foundation.   This chapter consists of two different piece configurations. In Section 6.1, we focus on knight against bishop endings, where in Section 6.2, our knight and pawns must neutralize a knight to reach their goal.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "034.002"]
[White "Introduction - The Unpredictable Knight - Knight against Bishop"]
[Black "6.1 The Unpredictable Knight - Knight against Bishop"]
[FEN "8/P7/4p3/4N3/8/1b2k3/8/7K w - - 0 4"]
[Result "*"]

{Bishops can only reach their potential when they get access to the right diagonals. This gives us two techniques that we can use to neutralize the enemy bishop and promote our pawn safely. We can either block the most important diagonal or ensure that the bishop cannot access it. We need both tools in the following exercise.   This position is reached in the final phase of a study that was composed by Sidorov in 1984. Our pawn has almost reached its destiny, but Black still has a little trick that prevents us from queening our pawn directly.} 4. Ng4+ {[%cal Gg4e3,Gg4f6,Gf6d5,Rb3d5,Ga7a8][%csl Re3,Rd5]We start with the targeting technique to manoeuvre our knight to f6 with tempo.
The trick we want to prevent is
4.a8=Q
Bd5+!
.} Kf4 {} 5. Nf6 {[%cal Gf6d5,Rb3d5][%csl Rd5]Now that we have the d5-square under control, Black must look for another way to tactically prevent our pawn from queening.} Bd1 {We are still unable to promote our pawn on account of 6...Bf3+. It is time to switch to Plan B and block the diagonal.} 6. Nd5+ {[%cal Gd1f3,Gf3a8,Gf3h1,Ga7a8][%csl Rd5,Ga8]} exd5 {} 7. a8=Q {Conclusion   Denying the bishop access to the diagonal or blocking the diagonal are important techniques that you can use to neutralize the defending bishop. Make sure you have them available when solving the exercises.   Good luck!} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
