{This section demonstrates that fighting a knight instead of a bishop requires a different set of skills. We can forget about diagonals, but should focus on luring the knight to an awkward square instead. Take, for instance, this sideline of a study that was composed by Sackmann in 1920.  Our d-pawn looks relatively innocent in this position. However, appearances can be deceiving and the picture changes completely after the next two moves.} 2. Nc7+ {[%cal Gc7e8,Gc7a6][%csl Ra6,Re8]First, we lure the knight to c7.} Nxc7 {} 3. d6 {[%cal Gd6d7,Gd6c7,Gd7d8][%csl Rc7,Gd8]And then it is time for our pawn to step in. The knight does not have access to the e6-square and is unable to stop the pawn independently. The only thing it can do is to call in the help of the king.} Kb7 {The king is on its way, but it turns out the proximity of the knight is doing more harm than good.} 4. d7 {[%cal Gd7c8,Gd7d8,Rb7c8,Rb7c7][%csl Gd8,Rc7,Rc8]We have already seen this motif in Chapter 2. The knight is very poorly placed and the reason that Black can safely resign here.   Conclusion   Manoeuvring the enemy knight into awkward situations is just one of the many techniques that you can use to solve the exercises correctly. For the others, you can either use your own creativity or rely on the lessons from Chapter 2.   Good luck!} 


