5. Nb7+ {First we lure the knight to b7.} Nxb7 {} 6. c5 {And then we make sure it cannot return to its original square! It is game over as Black can no longer stop our e-pawn.} Nd8 {} 7. e7 {Rinck (1915).} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "036.002"]
[White "Introduction - The Selfless Bishop"]
[Black "Part II - Chapter 7. The Selfless Bishop"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{It is time to move on to the bishop, the final piece that we can train in this second part. The bishop is a real team player that often shines in cooperation with other pieces. You can also characterize it as selfless, as the following section is packed with beautiful examples in which the bishop sacrifices itself to allow another piece to excel.   Promote your pawn   Bishop sacrifices are not only common in positions with multiple pieces. They can also be very effective for queening your pawn! This chapter focuses exclusively on bishop against bishop endings in which our bishop puts itself completely at the service of one or more passed pawns.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "037.002"]
[White "Introduction - The Selfless Bishop - Promote your Pawn"]
[Black "7.1 The Selfless Bishop - Promoting your Pawn"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{Having a bishop available is a luxury that we did not have in Chapter 2 when we had to battle the enemy bishop with only our pawns. However, this does not mean that the exercises get any easier. On the contrary! The presence of an extra bishop gives us more options and therefore adds more complexity to the positions.   Two powerful techniques   The good news is that we can rely on many of the techniques that we have practiced before. I will show you two examples of a bishop against bishop ending where this is the case.   In the first one, we need to block a diagonal to prevent the enemy bishop from stopping our pawn. In the second one, we will overpower this piece by creating a second passed pawn.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "037.003"]
[White "1. Blocking the diagonal"]
[Black "7.1 The Selfless Bishop - Promoting your Pawn"]
[FEN "8/8/P1p3p1/1p1b1p2/3P3k/4K2B/4P3/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{This is a study that was composed by Lindgren in 1917. Our aim is to prevent Black from opening up the diagonal and stopping our pawn with 1...c5.} 1. Bg2 {[%cal Rd5g2]Although we can only enjoy our bishop for one move, it does play an important role. This sacrifice lures the black bishop away from the centre to enable us to block the long diagonal later.} Bxg2 {} 2. d5 {[%cal Rg2d5,Ge3d4,Gd4c5]We immediately force the bishop back into the centre. A motif that we have seen before. Our biggest achievement is that we cleared the d4-square for our king.} Bxd5 {The alternatives would not have been an improvement.
2...cxd5
enables us to block the diagonal with
3.Kd4
and
2...c5
3.d6
gives us a decisive second passed pawn.} 3. Kd4 {[%cal Gd4d5,Rc6c5,Ga6a8][%csl Rd5,Ga8]We immediately attack the bishop to prevent ...c6-c5.} Bh1 {} 4. Kc5 {[%cal Gh1a8,Ga6a8][%csl Rc6,Ga8]The final step was to block the pawn. With the diagonal closed, we can safely promote our pawn and win the game.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "037.004"]
[White "2. Creating a second passed pawn"]
[Black "7.1 The Selfless Bishop - Promoting your Pawn"]
[FEN "3B4/8/3p2P1/p3b3/P7/7p/2k5/7K w - - 0 3"]
[Result "*"]

{In this position, which is the final part of a study composed by Bernhardt in 1963, we use our bishop to create a second passed pawn that will decide the game.} 3. Bxa5 {[%cal Ga4a8,Gg6g8][%csl Ga8,Gg8]The first step looks easy. We simply pick up an extra pawn for free!} Kb3 {[%cal Gb3a4][%csl Ra4]} 4. Bc3 {[%cal Re5c3,Ga4a8,Gg6g8][%csl Ga8,Gg8]This bishop sacrifice was the key move. It was necessary to move away the bishop with tempo to keep our a-pawn safe from the black king.} Bxc3 {4...Kxc3
5.a5
wins in a similar fashion to the main line.} 5. a5 {[%cal Rc3a5,Gg6g8][%csl Gg8,Gg7]White wins because the black king stands outside the pawn's square and the bishop is unable to stop two passed pawns all by itself.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
