[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "041.002"]
[White "Introduction - Rook and Bishop"]
[Black "Part III - Chapter 9. Rook and Bishop"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{In Part III, we will slightly increase the level of complexity by adding a second piece to our squad. We kick off with my favourite chapter in this book: The rook and bishop combination.   Perfect match   The rook and bishop complement each other incredibly well. Together, they can attack from a large distance along ranks, files, and diagonals. Just like a queen! On top of that, they can attack a target twice or form a battery without the help of the king or a pawn.   About this chapter   This chapter deals with two different kinds of piece configurations. We start with the rook and bishop against a rook and a minor piece.   In sections 9.1-9.3, our aim is to win a piece, mate the king, or win the game. Just like we did before.  Section 9.4 teaches us to defend ourselves, either to survive, or to win.   The real fun starts in Section 9.5, when our rook and bishop must battle a queen! Prepare yourself for highly instructive and entertaining exercises in which our rook and bishop must find the perfect coordination to outsmart this mighty piece.   In short, we have the following outline for this chapter:   9.1 Winning a piece   9.2 Mating the king    9.3 Winning the game    9.4 Defending    9.5 Winning the queen} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "042.002"]
[White "Introduction - Rook and Bishop - Winning a Piece"]
[Black "9.1 Rook and Bishop - Winning a Piece"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{We start with endings where our rook and bishop play against a rook and a minor piece. Our goal in this section is to get a decisive material advantage by either winning a piece or an exchange.   I have prepared two examples that demonstrate powerful tactical tools. These are the interference technique and the eternal pin.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "042.003"]
[White "1. Interference"]
[Black "9.1 Rook and Bishop - Winning a Piece"]
[FEN "r6k/1p4Rp/2p4B/p2p4/P7/7P/P2Pn3/7K w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{This first example features the interference technique, which removes the defence from an enemy piece.   A brief look at this position reveals that the black knight is far from home and currently unprotected. We use an attack on this vulnerable piece to build a battery that threatens the enemy king. With tempo! A typical example of the targeting technique.} 1. Rg2 {[%cal Gg2e2,Gh6g7,Gg7h8,Gg2g7][%csl Re2,Rh8]} Re8 {This is Black's most stubborn defence. Removing the knight with
1...Nc1
would enable us to build a battery and execute the discovered attack with tempo after
2.Bg7+
Kg8
3.Bb2+
Kf7
4.Bxc1
.} 2. Bg7+ {[%cal Gg7h8,Rh8g8,Gg2g8][%csl Rh8,Rg8]We use the targeting technique for a second time to build up the other half of the battery with tempo.} Kg8 {Note that our action chases the enemy king to an unfavourable square as well.} 3. Be5+ {[%cal Ge8e2,Gg2g8,Gg2e2][%csl Re5,Re2,Rg8]This interference combination deprives the black knight from its protection and decides the game.} Kf7 {} 4. Rxe2 {White wins.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "042.004"]
[White "2. The eternal pin"]
[Black "9.1 Rook and Bishop - Winning a Piece"]
[FEN "7k/2p2p1p/5np1/8/8/BP1r3P/6PK/R7 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{This example features the eternal pin, which is a pin that cannot be removed by the opponent. This is a very elegant tactical resource for our toolbox that can be executed by either the rook or the bishop. In this case, our bishop will be the piece that remains on the board to keep the enemy piece pinned.} 1. Bb2 {[%cal Gb2h8][%csl Rh8,Rf6]We start with a regular pin. Black is forced to protect the knight.} Rd6 {1...Kg7
. White now creates an eternal pin on either the black rook or black knight. Both scenarios lead to the same result with the exact same technique:
2.Ra6
The interesting thing in this line is that White creates an eternal pin on the knight instead of the rook after
2.Rf1
transposes to our main line after
2...Rd6
3.Rxf6
Rxf6
2...Rd6
3.Rxd6
cxd6
4.h4
h6
5.b4
g5
6.h5
. Black is unable to break the pin and will soon end up in zugzwang. Note that White does not even have to win the knight as the b-pawn can simply decide the game.} 2. Rf1 {[%cal Gf1f6,Gb2h8][%csl Rf6,Rh8]} Kg7 {} 3. Rxf6 {[%cal Gb2g7,Rd6f6][%csl Rf6,Rg7]This is the key move. We sacrifice an exchange to eventually win back a rook.} Rxf6 {} 4. h4 {[%cal Gb2g7][%csl Rf6,Rg7]White successfully created an eternal pin on the black rook. Next, he can attack it with g2-g4-g5 or simply make waiting moves with his king until Black ends up in zugzwang and must move his king.
4.g4
allows Black to break the pin for one move, but leads to the same result after
4...g5
5.h4!
5...Kg6
6.h5+!
6...Kg7
7.b4
c6
8.Kg3
h6
9.Kg2
Kg8
10.Bxf6
.} h6 {4...h5
is another way to prevent g2-g4-g5, but loses after
5.Kh3
c5
6.Kh2
.} 5. b4 {[%cal Gb2g7][%csl Rg7,Rf6]} g5 {} 6. h5 {[%cal Gh5g6,Gb2g7][%csl Rg6,Rg7,Rf6]} c6 {} 7. g4 {[%cal Gb2g7][%csl Rf6,Rg7]Black is in zugzwang and his king must abandon the rook.} Kf8 {} 8. Bxf6 {This ending is an easy win for White.} 


