{The course 1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Beginners offered many different mate-in-one and mate-in-two combinations. The mate in ones familiarized you with the most important mating patterns. The mate-in-two combinations introduced the thinking process for solving more complex mating exercises.   The following two steps were trained extensively:   1. Identify the
(mating)
pattern   2. Find the right preparatory move.   Longer mating combinations   In this section, we will stretch our horizon by solving exercises that require you to look three or four moves ahead. Very often, you will need the chasing, targeting, and luring techniques to reach your goal.   In this Introduction, I will show you an example where we can create a mate by placing our rook close to the king. A very powerful technique that we have already discussed in 1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Beginners.   Our pieces are very remote in this exercise, but this is going to change very soon!} 1. Bf8+ {[%cal Rb4a5,Gf8b4][%csl Rb4]This move brings the bishop closer with tempo (targeting) and chases the black king towards the rim.} Ka5 {1...Ka4
would already be mate after
2.Ra7
.} 2. Ra7+ {[%cal Ga7a5,Gf8a3][%csl Ra5]Another combination of the targeting and chasing technique. We bring up our rook with tempo and force the black king into our mating net.} Kb6 {This is the position we were aiming at. Note how we manoeuvred our rook right next to the king and how it controls the square on the a-file and the seventh rank. It is now up to our bishop to decide the game at once.} 3. Bc5 {[%cal Gc5a7,Ga7c7,Ga7a5][%csl Ga7,Rb6]It is checkmate! Our bishop protects the rook and attacks the king at the same time.   Exercises  Remember this beautiful position. I can already tell you that this motif will return frequently in the upcoming exercises. Our aim will be to mate the king in three, or in four moves.   Good luck!} 


