{After three sections of learning and practicing how to coordinate your rook and bishop to win the game, it is now time to defend ourselves against our opponent's threats.   In this introduction, I will briefly discuss several techniques that can help you to come up with the optimal defence:   - Creating an evacuation square for your king   - Protecting your weakest spot   - The counterattack} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "045.003"]
[White "1. Creating an evacuation square"]
[Black "9.4 Rook and Bishop - Defend"]
[FEN "r1b3B1/pp2R2p/2p3p1/7k/5P2/6Kp/P1P5/8 b - - 0 26"]
[Result "*"]

{This position was reached in the 1992 game between Alexander van Beek and Richard Berendsen, played in the open Dutch Youth Championships under 12 in Hengelo.   Black has to be very precise in this position as 27.Rxh7 would be a direct mate. He can only survive when he creates an evacuation square.} 26. g5 {[%cal Re7h7,Gh5g6,Rh7h5,Gg6g5][%csl Gg6]After this move, the black king can breathe a sigh of relief as it has the g6-square available and won't be mated in the near future.
This is in sharp contrast to the game, which continued with
26...h6
. This is a seemingly logical move that misses a lethal combination. White rose to the occasion and unpacked the present with
27.Re5+!
Bf5
28.Rxf5+
gxf5
29.Bf7
.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "045.004"]
[White "2. Protecting your weakest spot"]
[Black "9.4 Rook and Bishop - Defend"]
[FEN "8/4R2p/n2r1ppk/p3p3/P3P1PP/1B6/4PK2/8 b - - 0 1"]
