{A brief look at this position might give you the impression that Black has no obvious weaknesses and can confidently play for a win. However, if we switch to our opponent's perspective, we can discover that White is on his way to checkmate us with
2.Bg8
and
2...--
3.Rxh7#
.  It turns out that
h7
is our most vulnerable spot that needs protection!} 1. Nc5 {[%cal Gc5d7,Gd6d7,Gd7h7][%csl Gd7,Gh7]This is by far the strongest move.
1...Rd8
is an important alternative to consider because it prevents 2.Bg8. The downside of the rook move is that it is very passive and gives White excellent winning chances after
2.g5+!
fxg5
3.hxg5+
Kxg5
4.Rxe5+
Kf4
5.Rxa5
.
1...g5
creates room for the black king, but does not solve the problem. White wins after
2.Be6!
gxh4
3.Bf5
h3
4.Rxh7+
Kg5
5.Rh5+
Kf4
6.e3
.} 2. Bg8 {} Rd7 {[%cal Gd7h7,Gc5d7][%csl Re7,Gh7,Gd7]This move solves Black's problems! The h-pawn is safe and the white rook will soon be expelled from the seventh rank. Most importantly, the game continues rather than ends for Black.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "045.005"]
[White "3. The counterattack"]
[Black "9.4 Rook and Bishop - Defend"]
[FEN "5R2/6p1/2B5/2p3b1/P3k1Pp/2K5/r5P1/8 b - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{In this last example, our king is under attack and we need to pick the right square to stay in the game.} 1. Ke5 {This was the right choice.
Black should definitely avoid
1...Ke3
2.Rf3+
Ke2
3.Bb5+
c4
4.Bxc4+
Ke1
5.Rf1
.} 2. Rf5+ {[%cal Gf5e5,Gf5g5][%csl Rg5,Re5]This double attack looks very threatening, but we have a powerful defensive resource available!} Kd6 {[%cal Gf5g5,Gd6c6][%csl Rg5,Rc6]The counterattack! We surrender our bishop only to win the other one in return.} 3. Rxg5 {} Kxc6 {We can be satisfied with an equal ending.} 


