[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "046.002"]
[White "Introduction - Rook and Bishop - Winning a Queen"]
[Black "9.5 Rook and Bishop - Winning a Queen"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{We conclude this chapter with my favourite section. The one where our rook and bishop have to overpower a queen! This mighty piece can move in any direction with incredible speed, so you can imagine that our rook and bishop must go the extra mile to catch it.   This Introduction shows you three examples in which I demonstrate the most important patterns we must know. In the first one, we learn that besides the skewer, the pin and discovered attack are our most important weapons for catching the queen, and we can often combine them to reach our goal.   The second example rehearses a technique we have encountered before. Placing the rook close to the king!   We finish this introduction with a brand new weapon that is tailor-made for the rook and bishop combination: the extended discovered attack.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "046.003"]
[White "1. Combining various tactical weapons"]
[Black "9.5 Rook and Bishop - Winning a Queen"]
[FEN "8/6K1/1p6/pB1q2k1/7p/2R1P3/P4P1P/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{In this first example, we must arm ourselves with multiple tactical weapons to push our opponent over the edge.} 1. f4+ {[%cal Gf4g5,Rg5f5,Rg5g4,Rg5h5][%csl Rg5]We start with a preparatory move that involves the chasing technique. Our aim is to force the enemy king and queen to squares that allow us to execute a deadly pin or a discovered attack.} Kf5 {The alternatives wouldn't have helped Black either:
1...Kg4
2.h3+
gives Black the difficult choice to lose with a pin, or with a discovered attack.
2...Kxh3
2...Kf3
3.Bc6
. The pin
3.e4+
. The discovered attack.
1...Kh5
is a forced mate after
2.Be2+
Qf3
3.Bxf3
.} 2. e4+ {[%cal Ge4f5,Ge4d5][%csl Rf5,Rd5]This is the second and final preparatory move. White lures either the black king or queen to e4, after which we decide the game with a deadly pin.} Qxe4 {[%cal Gb1h7][%csl Re4,Rf5]2...Kxe4
3.Bc6!
is similar.} 3. Bd3 {[%cal Gd3f5][%csl Re4,Rf5]The queen is pinned and will be lost on the next move.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "046.004"]
[White "2. Rook close to the king"]
[Black "9.5 Rook and Bishop - Winning a Queen"]
[FEN "8/4B2p/8/P4p2/5k1P/2R4P/KP3q2/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The pin and discovered attack have a prominent role in the previous example. This time, we must pick up the skewer and combine it with another powerful technique: placing the rook next to the king.  It seems like the black king and queen currently enjoy a lot of freedom. This is about to change dramatically.} 1. Re3 {[%cal Ge7g5,Gg5e3,Ge3e5,Ge3g3][%csl Rf4,Ge3]This is one of my favourite moves in this chapter. It is very hard to spot if you haven't seen it before. We threaten mate on the next move and capturing the rook, whether with the king or the rook, will either way be punished with a skewer.} h6 {[%cal Gh6g5,Re7g5][%csl Rg5]This is the best attempt as both
1...Kxe3
2.Bc5+
Ke2
3.Bxf2
and
1...Qxe3
2.Bg5+
Kf3
3.Bxe3
Kxe3
4.a6
give White an easy win.

Note that
1...Qc2
2.Bg5
is mate!
After the text move, our mate threat is parried, but instead we can chase the king to the queen's diagonal and finish the game with a skewer.} 2. Bd6+ {[%cal Gd6f4,Rf4e3][%csl Rf4]} Kxe3 {} 3. Bc5+ {[%cal Gc5f2][%csl Re3,Rf2]} Kf3 {} 4. Bxf2 {} Kxf2 {} 5. a6 {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "046.005"]
[White "3. The extended discovered attack"]
[Black "9.5 Rook and Bishop - Winning a Queen"]
[FEN "8/8/p5p1/4R3/5B1k/1Pq4P/K3P3/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The discovered attack is an important weapon in the fight against the queen. We should already know how a discovered attack requires a battery, which consists of a front piece and a back piece. This example shows you a more powerful version of this deadly device: the extended discovered attack.   This combination consists of two consecutive discovered attacks. The first one is aimed at reversing the battery, which means that it turns the back piece into a front piece and vice versa. With tempo!   The second discovered attack decides the game. The extended discovered attack is a very elegant technique that underlines the potential power of a well-coordinated rook and bishop. It also teaches us that a battery is most effective with the rook as the front piece.} 1. Bg5+ {[%cal Gg5h4,Rh4h5][%csl Rh4]We create the first battery with the bishop as the front piece and the rook as the back piece. Note how both chasing and targeting play an important role in this combination. First, we chase the black king to h5.} Kh5 {1...Kxh3
2.Re3+
wins the queen with a plain double attack.} 2. Bf6+ {[%cal Ge5h5,Rh5h6,Gf6c3][%csl Rh5,Rc3,Ge5]With the first discovered attack, we manoeuvred the bishop to f6 with tempo (targeting) and created our second battery. This time aimed against the queen.   Note how both pieces switched roles. The rook is now the front piece and the bishop the back piece. Black will soon experience that this is the most dangerous division of roles.} Kh6 {} 3. Rh5+ {[%cal Gf6c3,Gh5h6][%csl Rc3,Rh6]Discovered attack number two wins the queen and decides the game.} Kxh5 {} 4. Bxc3 {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "046.006"]
[White "Conclusion - Rook and Bishop - Winning a Queen"]
[Black "9.5 Rook and Bishop - Winning a Queen"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{In this section, we discovered that the pin, skewer and discovered attack should be the weapons of our choice if we want to outplay a queen with our rook and bishop. In most cases, we must prepare these tactics with the preparatory techniques targeting, chasing and luring.   Another powerful technique that we rehearsed is to create a mating net by placing the rook close to the king.   We finished this Introduction with the extended discovered attack, which consists of two consecutive discovered attacks. The first one is aimed at reversing the battery and the second at winning the queen.   Exercises   It is now time to solve highly entertaining exercises in which we can either catch the enemy queen directly or force it to surrender itself by creating threats against the king. In most cases, we need to play forcefully, but don't forget to insert a silent move when necessary.   Good luck!} 1. -- {} 


