[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "047.002"]
[White "Introduction - Two Major Pieces"]
[Black "Part III - Chapter 10. Two Major Pieces"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{In this chapter, we need to coordinate two major pieces to outplay our opponent.   We start with a queen and a rook in Section 10.1. As this is the most powerful two-piece combination available, we will be able to practice finishing the game with great force.   Slightly more subtle play is required in Section 10.2 when we must align two rooks to reach our goal.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "048.002"]
[White "Introduction - Two Major Pieces - Queen and Rook"]
[Black "10.1 Two Major Pieces - Queen and Rook"]
[FEN "2R5/8/1Q6/K7/p4q2/5r2/4k3/8 w - - 0 9"]
[Result "*"]

{The queen and the rook form the most dangerous piece combination that you can have in your own games. They are so powerful that they can decide games instantly, almost from out of nowhere. Sometimes this happens in many different ways. However, when the positions get more complex, or when the opponent has capable defenders around, we must still be very precise and equipped with the right techniques to bring the full point home.   Building a battery  In this Introduction, I have selected an example in which we overpower the enemy king on an almost empty board by building a battery. The main line and the various sidelines demonstrate different techniques that you can apply later in the exercise section.    This is the final phase in a study that was composed by Gromov and Pervakov in 2020. With only one pawn on the board, you can imagine that both kings are highly vulnerable to an attack. However, they also have a lot of manoeuvring space, which means that it is not so easy to create a checkmate. With many options for both sides, there is no point in calculating every possible line until the end. What we should do is try to bring our pieces closer to the king and create the most dangerous set-up that we can find.} 9. Rc2+ {[%cal Gc2e2,Re2d3][%csl Re2]This is the only move that gives White a winning advantage. White should definitely start with a forcing move. Preferable one that prevents the king from hiding between its defenders.} Kd3 {This reply is forced, as any king move towards the back rank is met with 10. Qb1+.} 10. Qb1 {[%cal Gb1d3,Gc2c8,Gc2h2][%csl Rd3]This silent move builds a dangerous battery that will soon enable us to continue our attack and parry all the threats with tempo. It was the key move that we had to find to solve this study. From here, most of the possible lines have more than one win for us which is why the remaining moves will be informational only. Nevertheless, I do recommend you to study them carefully, as they teach you valuable techniques that you can use in the exercise section.} Kd4 {The king travels upwards in an attempt to escape from our major pieces. This will soon turn out to be a hopeless mission, as our queen and rook are about to team up and demonstrate a classic ladder mate.  There were many different alternatives for Black that are interesting to analyse. I will show you the five
(!)
most important ones.
A.
10...Rf1
The rook attacks our back piece in an attempt to neutralize the battery.
11.Rc1+!
. In response, we use the discovered attack to go after the black rook!
11...Ke2
12.Qb5+
. This skewer chases the defending king away and decides the game.
B.
10...Ke3
This is another way to step out of the battery. We can, however, easily chase the king to the fourth rank to win the queen with a skewer.
11.Qc1+
Kd3
12.Qd1+
Ke4
12...Ke3
13.Re2
13.Rc4+
C.
10...Qe5+
In an open position with such powerful pieces, we must always consider an attack on our own king. Luckily, we still have our battery in place to stay on top with a counterattack!
11.Rc5+
D.
10...Qd4
This queen move is aimed against the skewer with Qc4 that we saw earlier. In response, we can relocate our rook with tempo and start an attack from the other side of the board.
11.Rh2+!
Kc4
Many moves win in this position, but the following is by far the most instructive one and therefore very suitable for this Introduction.
11...Kc3
12.Qc2
11...Ke3
12.Qe1+
Kf4
13.Rh4+
12.Rh4!
. The rook lures the queen away from the king to prepare a deadly skewer.
12...Qxh4
13.Qb4+
Kd5
14.Qxh4
E.
10...Qe3
This last alternative can be refuted in many different ways. The easiest one is to chase the king towards the e-file to prepare a pin:
11.Qd1+
Ke4
12.Re2
.} 11. Qb2+ {[%cal Gb2d4][%csl Rd4]This is, of course, not the only move, but certainly an instructive one. Since Black's main defender (the queen) is on a dark square, we want to rearrange our major pieces in a way that allows us to use the light squares on the b- and c-file for carrying out the mating combination.
In comparison, after the straightforward
11.Qb4+
Kd5
12.Rc5+
Ke6
13.Qb6+
Kd7
, White is still completely winning, but cannot simply complete the ladder mate manoeuvre as the c7-square is protected by the queen.} Kd5 {11...Kd3
12.Rc3+
Ke4
13.Rc4+
also wins for White.} 12. Qb5+ {[%cal Gb5d5,Rd5e6,Gc2c6][%csl Rd5]} Ke6 {The king cannot move backwards because
12...Ke4
13.Rc4+
loses the queen.} 13. Rc6+ {[%cal Gb5h5,Gc6h6][%csl Re6]It becomes visible how using the light squares for our pieces makes life much easier. We can simply continue our ladder mate and collect the full point.} Ke7 {} 14. Qb7+ {[%cal Gb7h7,Gc6h6][%csl Re7]} Kd8 {} 15. Rc8 {[%cal Gc8h8,Gb7h7][%csl Rd8]Conclusion   The queen and rook can form an extremely powerful team that can easily push the enemy king into any desired direction and finish the game with a skewer, discovered attack, or a (ladder) mate.  Exercises   I have created a set of exercises in which you can practice how to get the most out of this dangerous duo. The aim of the exercises is to win the game, which means that we must either mate the king or acquire a decisive material advantage.   Good luck!} 


