{This section contains a large set of exercises in which we must beat our opponent in a double rook ending. It is a balanced mix of positions in which we either have to win a rook, promote our pawn, or mate the king.   I will demonstrate each of these purposes in an example before I give the floor to you.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "049.003"]
[White "1. Winning a rook"]
[Black "10.2 Two Major Pieces - Two Rooks"]
[FEN "r7/p4R2/8/3pk2p/5R2/P2K1P2/1r6/8 w - - 0 32"]
[Result "*"]

{This first example features a game in which my highly esteemed editor Frank Erwich was able to win a rook against the future world-class player Jorden van Foreest. It was played in the Dutch team Championships in 2013.  A closer look at this position reveals Black's unprotected rooks and highly vulnerable king. Our aim will be to chase the king to the b-file to decide the game with a skewer.} 32. R4f5+ {[%cal Gf5e5,Re5d6][%csl Re5]We must keep a rook on the seventh rank to be able to carry out the skewer later.
32.R7f5+
is only a draw after
32...Kd6
33.Rf6+
Kc5
.} Kd6 {32...Ke6
33.R5f6+
Ke5
34.f4
is mate.} 33. R5f6+ {[%cal Gf6d6,Rd6c5][%csl Rd6]Black already knew what was coming and resigned in this position. He would lose a rook after} Kc5 {33...Ke5
34.f4} 34. Rc7+ {[%cal Gf6a6,Gc7c5,Gc7b7,Gb7b2,Rc5b5][%csl Rc5]} Kb5 {} 35. Rb7+ {[%cal Gb7b2][%csl Rb2,Rb5]} Kc5 {} 36. Rxb2 {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "049.004"]
[White "2. Promoting your pawn"]
[Black "10.2 Two Major Pieces - Two Rooks"]
[FEN "8/2P5/p1rr3k/8/4R1pK/R1p5/8/8 w - - 0 2"]
[Result "*"]

{Positions with double rooks can contain very similar patterns to the ones we discussed in the single rook endings, especially when we have to promote our pawn.   This is the final part of a study that was composed by Prokes in 1949. Our aim is to eliminate the c6-rook to safely promote our c-pawn.} 2. Rxa6 {[%cal Ga6h6,Gc7c8][%csl Rc6,Rd6,Rh6,Gc8]This is a pattern that you should recognize. The black rook cannot leave the c-file, nor can it capture our pawn! The only difference with a single rook ending is that the rook is currently protected! This means that it can ignore our last move and play something useful.} c2 {[%cal Gc7c8,Gc2c1]Luckily for us, we also have a second rook! And that one is about to decide the game.} 3. Rxc6 {3.c8=Q
Rxc8
4.Rxd6+
Kh7
is only a draw because White has to give up a rook for the passed c-pawn.} Rxc6 {} 4. Re6+ {[%cal Rc6e6,Gc7c8]This is another familiar pattern. We lure the rook away from the c-file and queen our pawn.} Rxe6 {} 5. c8=Q {[%cal Gc8c1][%csl Gc1]White wins as Black is not even close to building a fortress.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "049.005"]
[White "3. Mating the king"]
[Black "10.2 Two Major Pieces - Two Rooks"]
[FEN "2K5/8/k7/8/4Rp2/r1p5/P6R/5r2 w - - 0 3"]
[Result "*"]

{In this final example, our rooks have to cooperate smoothly to create a mating net around the black king.   This is the final part of a study that was composed by Horwitz in 1861. We must find a series of eight
(!)
moves to reach our goal. That sounds harder than it is. All we need to do is start with a ladder mate procedure and finish the game with a silent move.} 3. Rh6+ {[%cal Gh6a6][%csl Ra6]It was important to keep a rook on the fourth rank to prevent the king from running away.
3.Re6+
Kb5
wins for Black!} Kb5 {} 4. Rh5+ {[%cal Gh5b5,Ge4a4,Rb5b6][%csl Rb5]Our rooks control adjacent files, which means that we can start a ladder mate manoeuvre that chases the king into the corner.} Kb6 {The king cannot approach our rook as
4...Kc6
5.Re6
is mate.} 5. Re6+ {[%cal Gh5a5,Ge6b6,Rb6a7][%csl Rb6]} Ka7 {} 6. Rh7+ {[%cal Gh7a7,Ge6a6,Ra7a8][%csl Ra7]} Ka8 {This was the position that we were heading for. It is now time to stop our forceful play and switch on the silent mode.} 7. Rb6 {[%cal Gb6b8,Gb8a8,Gh7a7][%csl Ra8]This is the key move, leading to a forced mate. All that is left for Black is to postpone the mate by sacrificing both rooks.} Rb1 {} 8. Rxb1 {} Rb3 {} 9. Rxb3 {} c2 {} 10. Rb8 {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
