{In this chapter, we will switch our focus from the major pieces to the minor ones and practice our tactical skills in endings where we either possess the bishop pair, a pair of knights, or a knight and a bishop. We will work with the following outline:   11.1 Two Bishops  11.2 Two Knights  11.3 Knight and Bishop  Each section consists of two different sets of exercises: one in which our minor pieces are opposed by two pieces of similar strength, and one where we must compete with only one enemy piece, but a very strong one. The queen!} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "051.002"]
[White "Introduction - Two Minor Pieces - Two Bishops"]
[Black "11.1 - Two Minor Pieces - Two Bishops"]
[FEN "k7/8/2K1p3/4P1pb/1p2Bp1n/1P6/3P3B/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{We start our minor piece adventure with the two bishops. These two pieces form a perfect team as they both can cover all the squares that the other cannot reach! The two bishops love open positions that enable them to travel with lightning speed and attack from a large distance.   My favourite tactical manoeuvre in double bishop endings is the steamrolling technique. I will show you one example of this powerful instrument before we move on to the exercises.  In 1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Beginners, I explained how we can use a steamroller as a metaphor for a technique that steadily pushes your opponent’s pieces towards a desired square. The ladder mate is an example where this technique is deployed by two major pieces. We can also apply it with our bishops.   In this example, we must steamroll the king towards a5, where it will be mated.} 1. Kc7+ {[%cal Ge4a8,Gc6c7][%csl Ra8]The first step is to open up the long diagonal for our light-squared bishop.} Ka7 {} 2. Bg1+ {[%cal Ge4a8,Gg1a7][%csl Ra7]Next, we bring up our dark-squared bishop with tempo and chase the king one step further.} Ka6 {} 3. Bd3+ {[%cal Gg1a7,Gd3a6][%csl Ra6]And again! Note how we consistently attack the king with one bishop and let the other control the adjacent diagonal to prevent it from moving backwards.} Ka5 {We have reached our goal and can decide the game in one move.} 4. Bb6 {[%cal Gb6a5,Gd3a6][%csl Ra5]Conclusion  Two bishops can form a very good match when they have enough space to manoeuvre. This enables them to move fast and attack their targets from a large distance. When they control adjacent diagonals, they can sometimes use the steamrolling technique to steadily push the enemy king into a mating net.   Exercises   It is now time to move on to the exercises, where our bishops either have to outsmart two minor pieces, or the queen. Our task is to win the game. This means that it would be wonderful if you could mate the king, but you can also be satisfied if you find a way to win a piece instead.  Good luck!} 


