{In this section, we will replace our pair of bishops with two knights and discover how these highly unpredictable pieces are the perfect team for tricking your opponent. You will be amazed to see how many squares two well-coordinated knights can control, either directly or tactically. To illustrate this, I have selected one example in which our cavalry completely dominates the enemy queen.  We look at a study that was composed by Rinck in 1928. It might be hard to imagine, but Black's centralized queen is about to run out of moves in this open position.} 1. Nd7 {[%csl Rd5]This subtle silent move puts Black in zugzwang!
1.Ndc6+
Kc5
2.Na6+
looks tempting, but Black can simply ignore the knights and get a winning advantage after
2...Kb6!
3.a5+
Kb7!
.} a6 {This is the best try since the king is stuck and the queen drops after any of its 19 (!) legal moves. I will show you the six most interesting ones. Note how we must constantly combine the chasing and targeting techniques to reach our goal.
1...Qa8
2.Ne6+
. We chase the king to d5, while directing our knight to c7 with tempo.
2...Kd5
3.Nc7+
1...Qa2
2.Nc6+
Kd5
3.Nb4+
1...Qg8
2.Nc6+
Kd5
3.Ne7+
1...Qh5
2.Ne6+
Kd5
3.Nf4+
1...Qxg2
2.Ne6+
Kd5
3.Nf4+
1...a5
2.b3!
puts Black in another zugzwang!} 2. a5 {[%csl Rd5]Now it is Black's turn again!} Qb5 {The queen tries to escape, but is an easy target for our knights.} 3. Ne6+ {[%cal Ge6d4,Rd4d5,Ge6c7,Gc7b5,Gc7d5][%csl Rd4,Rd5,Rb5]The knight chases the king to d5, while moving closer to c7 with tempo.} Kd5 {} 4. Nc7+ {[%cal Gc7b5,Gc7d5][%csl Rd5,Rb5]} Kc6 {} 5. Nxb5 {Conclusion  This example demonstrated how two knights can control many different squares by combining the chasing and targeting techniques. Remember that when you are able to coordinate these beautiful pieces, you can even dominate a queen in an open position!   Exercises  It is now time to train our skills! We can first warm up with a small set of mate-in-three exercises, before we move on to the more challenging section where our knights must compete with a queen.   Good luck!} 


