[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "053.002"]
[White "Introduction - Two Minor Pieces - Knight and Bishop"]
[Black "11.3 - Two Minor Pieces - Knight and Bishop"]
[FEN "8/5N1p/6p1/1p1k4/1P6/2P3Pq/2B2P2/6K1 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{We have arrived at the last combination of two minor pieces. The bishop and the knight! This means that we can finally create a battery and execute discovered attacks with our pieces, which was not possible with a pair of knights or bishops.   The knight and bishop can be a fantastic team, as long as they divide the tasks effectively. An important technique could be to assign the control of the dark squares to the knight when you possess a light-squared bishop. I have prepared an example to show you what this looks like in practice.  This study was composed by Platov in 1914. Our aim is to combine attacks on the two black pieces to ultimately win the queen. We can only be successful when we find the perfect coordination between our bishop, knight and pawns.} 1. Bb3+ {[%cal Gb3e6,Rd5c6,Ge6h3][%csl Rd5,Rh3]The first step is to chase the king towards a less favourable square. We benefit from the fact that our knight is already located on the same colour as our bishop and therefore in control of the (dark) squares that the other piece cannot cover.} Kc6 {The only move as
1...Ke4
2.Ng5+
immediately loses the queen.} 2. Be6 {[%cal Rh3e6,Gf7d8,Gd8c6,Gd8e6][%csl Re6,Rc6]The second step is to chase the queen to h5.} Qh5 {This is the only move as
2...Qxe6
3.Nd8+
wins the queen and gives White a winning pawn ending. Note that all of our pawns are located on the dark squares and therefore also very well aligned with our bishop.} 3. Bg4 {[%cal Rh5g4,Gf7e5,Ge5c6,Ge5g4][%csl Rg4,Rc6]And this is attack number three! We are almost there.} Qd5 {3...Qxg4
4.Ne5+
wins the queen already.} 4. Bf3 {[%cal Gf3c6][%csl Rc6,Rd5]And this is an offer our opponent cannot refuse. It is game over.} Qxf3 {} 5. Ne5+ {[%cal Ge5f3,Ge5c6][%csl Rf3,Rc6]Conclusion  The knight and bishop can become a well-oiled machine when they divide the tasks well. This means that if you possess a light-squared bishop, you should assign the dark squares to the knight. And do the same with your pawns!   Exercises  Just like in the previous section, I have prepared a small set of mate-in-three exercises as a starter. The comprehensive main course will be to outplay a queen!   Good luck!} 


