[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "054.002"]
[White "Introduction - Queen and Minor Piece"]
[Black "Part III - Chapter 12. Queen and Minor Piece"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{This chapter consists of two sections in which we combine a queen with a minor piece.   We kick off with the queen and knight duo in Section 12.1 and learn that these two pieces complement each other perfectly.   Coordinating a queen and a bishop is usually slightly more challenging, but Section 12.2 shows us that these pieces can create powerful tactics as well.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "055.002"]
[White "Introduction - Queen and Minor Piece - Queen and Knight"]
[Black "12.1 Queen and Minor Piece - Queen and Knight"]
[FEN "3q4/2p3pk/p6p/3nN3/1p6/4P1QP/1P4P1/6K1 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The unpredictable knight is the only piece that adds real, distinctive qualities to the team when joining forces with the mighty queen. I always love to have these two pieces around in my own games. I consider them the perfect candidates for turning the tables with an unexpected tactic when your position seems hopeless. To illustrate this, I have created a position in which we are a pawn down, but have the opportunity to outplay our opponent with a little queen and knight magic.  Our aim in this position is to improve both our queen and knight with tempo before we strike with a tactic.} 1. Qg6+ {[%cal Gg6h7,Rh7g8,Gg6e6][%csl Rh7,Ge6]We start with the targeting technique. The queen uses the vulnerable black king to get closer with tempo.} Kg8 {The only move as
1...Kh8
2.Nf7+
is a simple double attack.} 2. Qe6+ {[%cal Ge6g8,Ge6f5,Rg8h7][%csl Rg8,Gf5]This time, we use the chasing technique to make progress. We want to have the black king on h7 to play the next move with check.} Kh7 {Note how our knight keeps playing an important role as it facilitates both
2...Kh8
3.Nf7+
and
2...Kf8
3.Ng6
.} 3. Qf5+ {[%cal Gf5h7,Rh7g8,Ge5c6,Gc6d8,Gf5d5][%csl Rh7,Rd8,Rd5]The queen has reached its dream location. With tempo!
3.Qg6+
does not help us any further as after
3...Kg8
, we must return to the previous position with
4.Qe6+
Kh7
.} Kg8 {The queen is ready, which means it is time to pass the baton to our knight.} 4. Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d8,Gf5d5,Gc6e7,Ge7g8,Ge7d5][%csl Rd8,Rd5,Rg8]The knight also uses the targeting technique to get closer to the desired square with tempo.} Qd6 {We have used a lot of targeting and a little chasing to get to this position. It is now time to decide the game with a typical luring combination.} 5. Qxd5+ {[%cal Gc6e7,Ge7g8,Ge7d5,Rd6d5][%csl Rg8,Rd5]} Qxd5 {} 6. Ne7+ {[%cal Ge7d5,Ge7g8][%csl Rd5,Rg8]} Kf7 {} 7. Nxd5 {White should win this ending without any problems by blocking the queenside pawns with the pawn and knight and picking them up with the king later.   Conclusion   The queen and knight can form a very tricky duo that is able to create a tactic out of nowhere. I have created two sets of exercises that you can use to apply the queen and knight magic yourself. In the first set, we need to mate the king, whereas in the second set, we only know that we must win the game.  As we get closer to the end of this course, I decided to insert a few quite challenging exercises. You can be really proud if you are able to solve them!   Good luck!} 


