2.d4
Qf8
3.Qh2
.} 2. d4 {} Qg5 {2...Qe7
3.Nf5+
.} 3. Qh2 {Troitzky (1895).} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "056.002"]
[White "Introduction - Queen and Minor Piece - Queen and Bishop"]
[Black "12.2 Queen and Minor Piece - Queen and Bishop"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{It is now time to replace the knight with a bishop and learn that a queen and bishop can form a powerful tactical partnership that dominates open positions.   I have created two examples that demonstrate how the queen and bishop can outplay the opponent through tactical coordination.   The first one involves a position with opposite-coloured bishops in which we take full control over one colour complex and drive the king into our mating net.   The second one is a bit more heroic as our bishop is on a mission to sacrifice itself to let the queen decide the game.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "056.003"]
[White "1. An attack with opposite-coloured bishops"]
[Black "12.2 Queen and Minor Piece - Queen and Bishop"]
[FEN "7k/5Bpp/2p2b2/7Q/8/6PP/1q3PK1/8 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{While opposite-coloured bishop endings are often drawish, they can easily be transformed into sharp, tactical battles when we add queens to the board. An old rule states that opposite-coloured bishops favour the attacker, as full control over one colour complex makes it easier to target the enemy king with threats that the defender can't match.   Taking full control over the light squares is our aim in the following study, which was composed by Del Rio in 1750.   Black's position looks relatively stable for the moment. Until we somehow force the h-pawn to a dark square and start dominating the light ones with our queen and bishop!} 1. Bg8 {[%cal Gg8h7,Gh5h7,Yh8g8,Yh5e8][%csl Rh7]This cute little tactic helps us to achieve a major positional goal, one that we can immediately exploit tactically.} h6 {1...Kxg8
2.Qe8
would not have been an improvement for Black.} 2. Qe8 {[%cal Ge8h8,Gg8f7,Gf7g6][%csl Rh8]The next step is to create a battery that we can use later to optimize our bishop with tempo.} Qe5 {[%cal Ge5e8][%csl Re8]Attacking the back piece is the best defensive try.
A move like
2...Bd4
would lose instantly after
3.Bf7+
Kh7
4.Bg6} 3. Be6+ {[%cal Ge5e8,Ge8h8][%csl Re6,Rh8]The bishop opens the back rank with tempo, signalling the start of a sequence that drives the black king straight into our mating net.} Kh7 {} 4. Qg8+ {[%cal Gg8h7,Rh7g6,Ge6g8][%csl Rh7,Gg8]} Kg6 {} 5. Bf7+ {[%cal Gf7g6,Gg8f7][%csl Rg6,Gf7]} Kf5 {We are almost there. All that is left is to manoeuvre our queen to g6 and accept our opponent's resignation.} 6. Qh7+ {[%cal Gh7f5][%csl Rf5]} Kg5 {} 7. Qg6 {This position depicts the success of our light-square strategy very well. Pay special attention to the poor black bishop on f6 that might as well have been glued to the board during this study.} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "056.004"]
[White "2. Sacrificing the bishop"]
[Black "12.2 Queen and Minor Piece - Queen and Bishop"]
[FEN "7Q/8/3pk2p/7P/8/p2q1B2/2p2P2/1bK5 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The following study, composed by Kubbel in 1927, is a very nice example of how the queen and bishop can dominate in an open position.   It is often hard to mate a centralized king in an open position because it has so many escape squares available. That is why we must go after the queen instead.} 1. Be2 {[%cal Ge2d3,Gh8e8,Ge8e2][%csl Rd3,Re6]The bishop sacrifice is the central theme of this study. There will be many occasions when we give up this piece to lure the black queen to a very uncomfortable square.} Qe4 {It makes sense to keep the queen close to the king.
The alternatives lose quickly. For instance:
1...Qb3
2.Qg8+
1...Qf5
2.Bg4
Qxg4
3.Qc8+
1...Qd5
2.Bc4
Qxc4
3.Qg8+
1...Qh3
2.Qc8+
1...Qxe2
2.Qe8+
.} 2. Qe8+ {[%cal Ge8e4,Re6d5,Re6f5][%csl Re4,Re6]} Kd5 {[%cal Gd5e4,Re8e4][%csl Ge4]2...Kf5
is a mirrored version of the main line. White wins after
3.Bd3
Qxd3
4.Qg6+
Ke5
5.Qxd3} 3. Bf3 {[%cal Re4f3,Ge8a8][%csl Rd5,Rf3]This time the bishop makes an offer they can't refuse. It is game over after} Qxf3 {} 4. Qa8+ {[%cal Ga8f3][%csl Rf3,Rd5]} Ke5 {} 5. Qxf3 {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "056.005"]
[White "Conclusion - Queen and Minor Piece - Queen and Bishop"]
[Black "12.2 Queen and Minor Piece - Queen and Bishop"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{This section demonstrated that opposite-coloured bishops tend to favour the attacker, as once the queen and bishop break through on a colour the defender cannot control, it often becomes impossible to hold the position.  Additionally, we examined an example where the bishop was sacrificed to displace the opposing queen, allowing us to win it with a precisely executed skewer.   Exercises  It is now time to practice the coordination between our queen and bishop in the upcoming exercise section, which consists of two different parts.   First, we must mate the king in a certain number of moves. Next, we will tackle exercises with no guidance other than the objective: to win the game.   Good luck!} 1. -- {} 


