{In this section, we’ll focus on the tactical elements of rook and knight endgames. This means that we can often use forks, skewers and mating nets to reach our goal.   We will practice two different kinds of piece configurations, starting with the rook and knight against a rook and a minor piece, and concluding with positions where our rook and knight must take on the challenge of facing a queen.   This chapter has the following outline:   13.1 Mating the king   13.2 Winning the game  13.3 Defending against rook and knight  13.4 Rook and knight against queen} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "058.002"]
[White "Introduction - Rook and Knight - Mating the king"]
[Black "13.1 Rook and Knight - Mating the King"]
[FEN "8/3p4/n7/6pk/5PN1/4PK2/rp6/3R4 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{We begin with the most straightforward exercises, where the objective is to mate the king to achieve victory. I have prepared an example in which we must cut off the king to prevent its escape.   We need to act quickly in this position, as our opponent is on the verge of queening the b-pawn. Fortunately, the king is now perfectly placed for a mating attack. If we hesitate, however, it may find an escape route via g6. This brings us to the next move.} 1. Rd6 {[%cal Gd6h6][%csl Rh6,Rg6]We need to cut off the king to prevent it from escaping our mating net. Since we threaten mate, there is no need to fear the black b-pawn for the moment.
1.f5
enables Black to defend with
1...Ra1!
.} gxf4 {The king is locked in our mating net and is no match for our attacking pieces.
1...b1=Q
2.Rh6
.} 2. Rh6+ {[%cal Gh6h5][%csl Rh5]} Kg5 {} 3. exf4+ {[%cal Gf4g5,Gh6h5][%csl Rg5,Rh5]} Kf5 {} 4. Ne3 {4.Rf6
is also mate.   Conclusion   A powerful technique for completing the mating net is to cut off the king along a rank. Once the king’s escape is restricted, it becomes much easier for the rook, knight, and any supporting pawns to deliver the final blow.   Exercises  A series of mate the king exercises is waiting for you. In most cases, the task is to deliver mate in three moves. We conclude with slightly more challenging exercises requiring mate in four.} 


