{In this section, we'll solve mixed exercises where the only instruction is to win the game. It will be up to you to decide whether to promote a pawn, win material, or deliver checkmate in a certain number of moves.   Before we begin, I’ll walk you through one example so you know what to expect.   This is a study that was composed by Pogosyants in 1979. With no pawns available, our pieces will have to do all the hard work themselves.} 1. Nf6+ {[%cal Gf6h7,Gg5f5,Gg4g8][%csl Rh7]We begin by optimizing our knight with tempo. The f6-square is the perfect spot, as it creates mating threats on both sides of the black rook.} Kg7 {1...Rxf6
2.Kxf6
. We already know that this is a win, since we are about to trap the king in the wrong corner with 3.Rg6.
1...Kh8
2.Rh4+
Kg7
3.Rh7+
Kf8
4.Rh8+
Kg7
4...Ke7
5.Re8
5.Rg8
.} 2. Kf5+ {[%cal Gg4g7][%csl Rg7]This check chases the black king towards a mating net of its own choice.} Kf8 {The king can run, but cannot hide! It is mate in two moves now.
2...Kh6
3.Rg6
2...Kh8
3.Rg8
.} 3. Rg8+ {[%cal Gg8f8,Gf6g8][%csl Rf8,Gg8]} Ke7 {} 4. Re8 {Conclusion   The key step in this exercise was placing the knight on the highly effective f6-square and recognizing the mating patterns that could decide the game.   In the upcoming mixed exercises, the main challenge will be to find similar coordination between your rook, knight, king, and pawns.   Good luck!} 


