{We have arrived at the final section in this rook and knight chapter. This means that it is again time to catch queens! Or mate the king when the opportunity arises.   Before diving into the exercises, I would like to show you two instructive examples where our rook and knight must compete with a queen.   The first is a composed study, where we restrict a centralized king by placing our rook right next to it.  The second is a practical example taken from an actual game. It highlights how these tactical themes not only occur in studies but can also easily arise in your own play.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "061.003"]
[White "1. Restricting a centralized king"]
[Black "13.4 Rook and Knight - Rook and Knight against Queen"]
[FEN "R7/1p4p1/2q4p/1N6/4kP2/6P1/2PPp3/4K3 w - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{The following study was composed by Platov in 1905. It features a prominent use of a technique we explored in detail earlier: placing your rook close to the enemy piece.   The black king and queen are centrally placed and still have quite some room to manoeuvre. We start with optimizing our rook with tempo.} 1. Re8+ {[%cal Ge8e4][%csl Re4]We use a little tactic to reach our goal.} Kd5 {The only move, as the alternative, runs straight into a knight fork.
1...Qxe8
2.Nd6+
1...Kf3
2.Nd4+
1...Kf5
2.Nd4+
.} 2. Re6 {[%cal Ge6c6,Ge6e4][%csl Rd5]Placing the rook close to the king! The rook remains untouchable, while the king’s breathing space is significantly reduced.} Qc4 {The alternative would, again, run into a basic tactic.
2...Qxe6
3.Nc7+
2...Kxe6
3.Nd4+
2...Qxb5
3.Re5+
2...Qxc2
3.Re5+
Kc4
4.Na3+
2...Qc8
3.Re5+
Kc4
4.Nd6+
.} 3. Re4 {[%cal Ge4e6,Ge4c4][%csl Rc4,Rd5]For the second time, we place the rook close to the king and chase the queen to an inferior square.} Qc6 {The queen retreats to where it came from. Notice how the black pieces remain on their original squares, while we have made significant progress. Our rook has advanced all the way from a8 to e4!
3...Kxe4
4.Nd6+
3...Qxe4
4.Nc3+
.} 4. d3 {[%cal Ge4e5,Ge5d5][%csl Rd5]This silent move is probably the hardest one to find in this study. The aim is to cover the c4-square and prepare 5.Re5#.} Qf6 {There alternatives lose the queen to a skewer.
4...Qd7
5.Rd4+
4...Kc5
5.Rc4+
4...Qb6
5.Re5+
Kc6
6.Re6+
.} 5. Re5+ {[%cal Ge5d5,Rd5c6][%csl Rd5]5.Nc7+!
Kc5
6.Rc4+
Kb6
7.Nd5+
would be an alternative route towards victory.} Kc6 {} 6. Re6+ {[%cal Ge6c6,Rf6e6,Gb5d4,Gd4e6,Gd4c6][%csl Rc6,Re6]This fourth (!) rook sacrifice is an offer Black cannot refuse. It is game over after} Qxe6 {} 7. Nd4+ {} Kd6 {} 8. Nxe6 {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "061.004"]
[White "2. Rook and knight vs queen in a practical game"]
[Black "13.4 Rook and Knight - Rook and Knight against Queen"]
[FEN "8/6p1/1p4k1/p2PR2p/2q1N2P/8/6PK/8 b - - 0 45"]
[Result "*"]

{Scenarios where a rook and knight outplay a queen aren’t just found in studies or training exercises. They also happen in real games, maybe even in your own! I have selected this game as an example.  This position was reached by Aleksander Czerwonski
(2358)
and Marek Chomczyk
(2274)
in Warsaw in 2019.  Black is completely winning in this position, with the straightforward plan of promoting a queenside pawn. Caution is still required, however, as White’s centralized pieces, combined with Black’s exposed king, could pose real danger.} 45. a4 {This careless move enables White to turn the tables completely.
It was essential to focus on the king's safety first with
45...Qd4!
, where White's attack fades out quickly after
46.Re6+
Kf7
47.Ng5+
Kf8
.} 46. Re6+ {[%cal Ge6g6][%csl Rg6]After this move, it becomes clear that the queen’s placement on c4 is highly unfortunate. Black resigned in view of} Kh7 {Black loses a queen after both
46...Kf7
47.Nd6+!
Kg8
48.Nxc4
and
46...Kf5
47.Nd6+
.} 47. Ng5+ {[%cal Gg5h7,Ge6h6][%csl Rh7]} Kg8 {} 48. Re8 {[%cal Ge8g8,Gg5f7,Gg5h7][%csl Rg8]} 


