[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "065.002"]
[White "Introduction -  Three-Piece Tactics"]
[Black "Part IV - Chapter 15. Three-piece Tactics"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{Now that we've explored every possible one- and two-piece combination, it's time to spice things up and introduce a third piece to the board. With three pieces, we can create more complex checkmates, achieve more refined coordination between the pieces, or even sacrifice one to allow the remaining two to deliver the final blow.   We begin this chapter with three-piece exercises where the task is to mate the king in three, four, or even more moves. From there, we move on to less clearly defined challenges, where success means simply finding a way to win the game.   The final section explores the fascinating struggle between three minor pieces against a queen, a battle full of subtle moves, where we must find the optimal coordination between our pieces.} 1. -- {} 


[Event "1001 Chess Endgame Exercises for Club Players"]
[Round "066.002"]
[White "Introduction - Three-Piece Tactics - Mating the King"]
[Black "15.1 Three-piece Tactics - Mating the King"]
[FEN "6k1/3R2p1/4n3/1pPp1p1Q/1P1PpP2/1KN1P1rP/1P6/q7 b - - 0 1"]
[Result "*"]

{Our aim in this section is to mate the king in a predefined number of moves. Earlier, I explained how exercises often become more complex when more moves must be visualized, but that other factors can also play a role. For example, the level of difficulty may depend on how forced the line is, or whether the tactical pattern is hidden at the start of the variation.   Hidden tactic   The following position is a good example of a short combination with a hidden tactic. One that’s easy to miss in a real game, especially with little time on the clock. However, things suddenly become much easier when someone prints it on a diagram for you and labels it with mate in two moves. Your trained eye would then immediately start looking for forced moves and come up with} 1. Nxd4+ {[%cal Re3d4,Gg3b3,Ga1a4][%csl Rc3,Rb3]This powerful knight sacrifice opens up the third rank with force.} 2. exd4 {The only move, as the knight did not only attack the king, but also controls the c2-square.  It suddenly becomes visible that the white knight is pinned along the third rank! We all know that a pinned piece is a poor defender, which means that we can finish the game in style.} Qa4 {[%cal Ga4b3,Gg3b3][%csl Rb3,Rc3]Conclusion   Although the complexity of tactical combinations depends on several factors, one thing is clear: the best way to improve is to solve as many exercises as possible! That's why a large set of mate the king challenges is now waiting for you.   We begin with mate in three moves and eventually move on to mate in four and more!   Good luck!} 


