{This section is packed with exercises where we don’t know in advance whether we’ll need to win a piece, mate the king, or promote a pawn. This brings us closer to real game situations and encourages us to rely on our own judgment and calculation skills.   Before we start, I would like to show you a tactical weapon that we did not encounter before because it usually requires the presence of three pieces to work effectively: the double discovered attack.   The double discovered attack  As you can see, this position features two distinct batteries, each with our rook as the front piece, and the queen and bishop as separate back pieces. The presence of two batteries means we can unleash two discovered attacks, potentially targeting three
(!)
different squares in a single move. At the moment, only the mating square h8 is under threat, so our task is to find a clever move that disrupts the coordination of the black pieces and creates additional targets for our double discovered attack.} 1. d6 {[%cal Gd6c7,Gd6e7][%csl Rc7,Re7]This is the move that we were looking for. Black is now forced to take, leaving both the queen and c8-rook unprotected.} Qxd6 {Now that we have created two additional targets, we are ready for our double discovered attack.} 2. Rc5 {[%cal Gc5c8,Gh2d6,Gh2h8,Gc3h8][%csl Rc8,Rd6,Rh8]After this powerful blow, Black is suddenly facing three (!) simultaneous threats; this is simply too much to handle.} Qxh2 {This logical move leads to a forced mate in three, but Black did not have much of a choice.} 3. Rxc8+ {[%cal Gc8g8][%csl Rg8]} Re8 {} 4. Rxe8+ {[%cal Ge8g8][%csl Rg8]} Kh7 {} 5. Rh8 {Conclusion   The presence of a third piece adds complexity to our task, but it also opens the door to creative and elegant solutions. Although the double discovered attack is a rare tactic, it holds great instructional value, as it builds on more familiar tactical themes. If you ever manage to use it in one of your own games, please do send it to me. I’d love to see it in action!  Exercises   The exercises in this section closely resemble real game situations and are far from easy. However, having solved nearly 900 exercises so far, I’m confident that you’re more than ready to take them on!   Good luck!} 


