[Result "*"]

{We have arrived at the last section before the final test starts. Buckle up, because we are going to battle a queen with three minor pieces! It is a demanding task that calls for precise coordination and tactical finesse. Therefore, I have prepared one example to guide you in the right direction.   This study was composed by Troitzky in  1896. Our three pieces are quite far apart, and it is not immediately clear how they can reach the queen. However, its position on the same d8-h4 diagonal as the king is a tactical clue we should notice, and it may lead us to the forced move with which we should begin our calculation.} 3. Bh4+ {[%cal Gh4d8][%csl Rd8,Rg5]This is by far the most forcing move in this position and also one that lures the king to the rim.} Kxh4 {Here, we should note that the king can only move to g5, which means that bringing our knight from b5 to f3 would lead to mate. Let’s give it a try!} 4. Nd4 {[%cal Gd4f3,Gg7h5,Gf3g5,Gf3h4][%csl Rh4]} Kg5 {This is the only move that prevents our mating threat, but it also walks straight into a relatively simple double attack.} 5. Nge6+ {[%cal Ge6d8,Ge6g5,Gd4e6,Rf7e6][%csl Rd8,Rg5,Re6]} fxe6 {} 6. Nxe6+ {[%cal Ge6d8,Ge6g5][%csl Rd8,Rg5]} Kf6 {} 7. Nxd8 {White wins.  Conclusion   Once again, the selfless bishop had to sacrifice itself so the others could finish the job. And here's a little spoiler alert. You’ll need to be ready to sacrifice quite a few more bishops if you want to solve the exercises successfully!   Good luck!} 


